export const KNOWLEDGE = "=== PAGE: index.html | Dymek | Advanced Equipment Distributor for Semiconductor, Optics & Precision Industries in Asia\nEst. 1989 Hong Kong 8 markets across Asia\n# Advanced equipment and engineering support for Asia’s precision industries\nFor more than 35 years, Dymek has connected manufacturers and researchers across Asia with the right technologies from Europe, the USA, Japan and South Korea, and supported them on the ground.\nSince 1989 we have grown from one Hong Kong office to 10 offices in 8 markets, supporting 19 industry and process areas for more than 35 years.\n## What are you working on?\nPick your area. We’ll show you what we can help with and where to go next.\n## Built for industries that demand accuracy\nThe same metrology, bonding, inspection or alignment technology can solve very different problems. Start from your industry.\n### Semiconductor Manufacturing & Advanced Packaging\nEquipment, software and engineering support from the first wafer process to the finished package.\n- Wafer Processing & Device Fabrication\n- Advanced Packaging & Assembly\n- Metrology, Inspection & Failure Analysis\n- Equipment Service & Fab Support\n### Optics & Photonics\nEquipment and engineering for optical manufacturing, metrology, alignment and test.\n- Precision Optics & Optical Manufacturing\n- AR/VR & Wafer-Level Optics\n- Photonics, Optoelectronics & Optical Interconnects\n- 03 Aerospace & MRO Inspection, NDT and repair\n- 04 AI & Data Center Hardware Packaging, optics and thermal\n- 05 Life Sciences & Medical Devices Microfluidics, optics and test\n- 06 Automotive & E-Mobility Power devices, sensors, batteries\n- 07 Data Storage & Magnetic Devices MRAM, heads and media test\n- 08 Electronics Assembly & Test Assembly, X-ray and reliability\n- 09 Displays & Optoelectronics MicroLED, OLED and optical films\n- 10 Photovoltaics & Solar Thin film, cells and modules\n- 11 Industrial & Precision Manufacturing Surface, form and dimension\n- 12 Research & Academia Labs, pilot lines and custom rigs\n## A reliable link between global technology and Asian industry\n### Practical technical understanding\nWe start from your requirement and your process, then match the technology to it.\n### Clear communication across cultures\nClear, timely communication between you, our team and the equipment manufacturer, across languages, cultures and time zones.\n### Long-term engagement\nWe build relationships that last, not one-off transactions.\n### Regional presence\nTeams on the ground in 8 markets to support installation, service and execution.\n## How a project moves forward\nFor non-standard applications, we combine instruments with fixtures, motion, handling, machine vision and software.\n- 01 /06 Feasibility testing Prove the method on your real parts, before anyone commits budget.\n- 02 /06 Measurement development Define recipes, tolerances and acceptance criteria together.\n- 03 /06 System design Mechanics, motion, handling, safety and software, drawn around your process.\n- 04 /06 Fabrication Fixtures, tooling and integration built and checked in-house.\n- 05 /06 Installation Commissioning and operator training on your site.\n- 06 /06 Production support Service, spares and continuous improvements for the life of the tool.\n## Working with leading equipment makers\n## Close to your fab, lab and production line\nHead office in Hong Kong, with teams in mainland China, Taiwan, Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand, Vietnam and the Philippines.\n### Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR Head office\nHead office\n### Beijing, China\n### Shanghai, China\n### Dongguan, China\n### Hsinchu, Taiwan\n### Penang, Malaysia\n### Singapore, Singapore\n### Pathum Thani, Thailand\n### Hanoi, Vietnam\n### Manila (Muntinlupa), Philippines\n## Guides for engineers and buyers\nPlain-language explainers on the processes and measurements behind advanced manufacturing.\nAdvanced Packaging\n### What Is Hybrid Bonding? Wafer-to-Wafer vs Die-to-Wafer Explained\nHybrid bonding joins two chips through copper pads and dielectric at the same time, with no solder bumps. Here is how it works, where it is used and what it takes to get yield.\n- Photonics Co-Packaged Optics (CPO) Explained: Why AI Data Centers Are Moving Optics onto the Package 8 Oct 2026 · 6 min read\n- Failure Analysis How Laser Sample Preparation Speeds Up Semiconductor Failure Analysis 29 Sep 2026 · 6 min read\n\n=== PAGE: about.html | About Dymek | Advanced Equipment Distributor in Asia Since 1989\n# About Dymek: Advanced Equipment Partner to Asia Since 1989\nFor more than 35 years, Dymek has supported Asia’s manufacturers and innovators with access to advanced industrial technologies and practical technical expertise.\n## A trusted partner in advanced equipment distribution\nFounded in Hong Kong in 1989, Dymek Company Ltd. has grown into a regional organization. We support industries that demand accuracy, consistency and technical reliability.\nOver the years we have expanded across Asia while keeping one consistent focus: helping organizations connect with the right technologies, and supporting them through complex industrial requirements.\n## From a single office to a regional network\nEach step of growth has been driven by one need: to stay closer to customers and principals, so we can respond faster and support them better across the region.\nEstablished in Hong Kong.\nExpansion across key Asian markets, including Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand, the Philippines, Taiwan and China.\nStrengthened our Southeast Asia presence with a branch in Vietnam.\nEV Group and Dymek formed the joint venture EV Group Malaysia Dymek Sdn. Bhd. in Penang to strengthen regional customer support. Source: EV Group press release\n## How we operate\nDymek works as a reliable link between global technology providers and industrial organizations across Asia. We emphasize four things.\n### Practical understanding\nOf technical requirements, from spec sheet to production floor.\n### Clear communication across cultures\nBetween all parties, you, our team and the manufacturer, across languages, cultures and time zones.\n### Long-term engagement\nRather than short-term transactions.\n### Regional presence\nThat supports execution on the ground.\n### Mission\nTo connect customers with advanced equipment solutions, while helping principals reach and serve markets across Asia effectively.\n### Vision\nTo remain a trusted regional partner that enables industries to adopt and apply advanced technologies with confidence.\n## The people behind Dymek\nOur team brings together professionals with technical knowledge, industry experience and regional understanding. Each person helps make sure communication is clear, requirements are understood and support stays consistent throughout every engagement.\nWe work as one coordinated team across regions. That keeps us aligned with both customers and principals at every stage.\n## A steady partner in a complex industry\nDymek’s strength comes from experience, regional presence and a team that understands both the technical and operational sides of the industry. We support decisions, enable execution and build relationships that last.\n\n=== PAGE: services.html | Equipment Sales, System Integration, Calibration & Service in Asia | Dymek\n# Equipment Sales, Integration & Service Support\nWe support you before, during and long after you buy: choosing the right technology, building systems around your part, and keeping equipment accurate and running.\nService\n### Metrology Equipment Sales\nWe connect you with measurement, inspection and process equipment from our principals in Europe, the USA, Japan and South Korea. Our engineers help you choose the right tool for your application, then support it on the ground.\nService\n### Custom System Integration\nSome applications don't fit a standard tool. We build semi- or fully automated systems around your part and process, combining proven instruments with robotics, motion, machine vision, fixtures and software.\nService\n### Calibration & Service\nEquipment only creates value while it runs well. Our regional service teams keep your tools accurate, available and supported for their full working life.\n## Keeping mature semiconductor tools productive\nRepair, refurbishment and replacement for vacuum pumps, RF generators and matching networks, legacy furnaces, plus relocation and spare parts.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: industries.html | Industries We Serve: Semiconductor, Optics, Aerospace, Automotive & More | Dymek\n# Industries We Serve Across Asia\nFrom wafer fabs and optics workshops to aerospace MRO and university cleanrooms. Choose your industry to see the processes, measurements and support we offer.\n## Semiconductor Manufacturing & Advanced Packaging\nFrom front-end wafer processing to advanced packaging, failure analysis and fab support.\nSemiconductor\n### Semiconductor Manufacturing & Advanced Packaging\nWafer processing, hybrid bonding, advanced packaging, metrology, failure analysis and fab support equipment for R&D and more.\nFront-end\n### Wafer Processing & Device Fabrication\nLithography, wafer bonding, plasma etch and deposition, furnaces, dicing, laser annealing, CMP and thin-film metrology for silicon, MEMS and more.\nBack-end\n### Advanced Packaging & Assembly\nHybrid bonding, wafer-to-wafer and die-to-wafer bonding, temporary bonding/debonding, die placement, thermocompression and more.\nYield & quality\n### Metrology, Inspection & Failure Analysis\nThin-film metrology, wafer AOI, X-ray/CT, scanning acoustic microscopy, probing, SEM/TEM and more.\nService\n### Equipment Service & Fab Support\nRepair, refurbishment and replacement of vacuum pumps, RF generators and matching networks, legacy furnace upgrades, relocation and more.\n## Optics & Photonics\nConventional precision optics, wafer-level micro/nano optics, and photonic devices and optical interconnects.\nOptics & Photonics\n### Optics & Photonics\nEquipment for precision optics, AR/VR waveguides and wafer-level optics, photonic devices and more.\nComponents\n### Precision Optics & Optical Manufacturing\nInterferometers, non-contact 3D surface metrology, thin-film measurement, lens centration and active alignment and more.\nMicro & nano optics\n### AR/VR & Wafer-Level Optics\nNanoimprint lithography, wafer-level lens molding and stacking, AR waveguide laser singulation, etch, deposition and metrology for AR/VR and more.\nPhotonic devices\n### Photonics, Optoelectronics & Optical Interconnects\nPhotonic device fabrication, wafer-level photonic integration, co-packaged optics and optical I/O, active alignment and more.\n## More industries\nSingle-page sectors where the same core technologies solve industry-specific problems.\nAerospace\n### Aerospace Manufacturing & MRO\nPortable 3D defect inspection for turbine blades, automated dimensional metrology, tribology and coating testing, failure analysis and more.\nAI & HPC\n### AI Computing & Data Center Hardware\nHybrid bonding for HBM and chiplets, co-packaged optics and optical I/O, advanced package inspection, failure analysis and more.\nLife sciences\n### Life Sciences, Diagnostics & Medical Devices\nElectron microscopy for biology, microfluidic and lab-on-chip fabrication, diagnostic substrate inspection, precision glass processing and more.\nAutomotive\n### Automotive & E-Mobility\nAutomated 3D inspection, tribology, EV battery X-ray/CT, SiC/GaN power semiconductor processing, MEMS sensors, LiDAR and camera optics alignment and more.\nData storage\n### Data Storage & Magnetic Devices\nMagnetic thin-film wafer mapping, disk media DLC metrology, head and slider measurement, MRAM characterization and more.\nElectronics\n### Electronics Assembly, Test & Reliability\nSMT and PCB inspection, X-ray and CT for electronics, laser microvia drilling, EMC pre-compliance, RF and sub-THz probing and more.\nDisplays\n### Displays & Advanced Optoelectronics\nMicroLED and miniLED laser lift-off, transfer and repair, large-area nanoimprint, panel and color-filter metrology and more.\nSolar\n### Photovoltaics & Solar Manufacturing\nDiffusion furnaces, PVD and plasma deposition, laser contact opening, thermal laser separation for half-cells and more.\nIndustrial\n### Industrial & Precision Manufacturing\nAutomated 3D inspection beyond CMM, portable surface gauges, machine vision AOI, tribology and coating testing, laser micro-drilling and more.\nResearch\n### Research & Academia\nElectron microscopy, micro- and nanofabrication, optics and photonics research tools, quantum device integration, 2D materials and more.\n## Can’t find your industry?\nOur technologies often apply beyond the sectors listed here. Tell us what you are making and we will tell you honestly whether we can help.\n\n=== PAGE: partners.html | Our Principals: EVG, 4D Technology, 3D-Micromac & More | Dymek\n# Our Principals\nWe work with equipment makers from Europe, the USA, Japan and South Korea, and help them reach and serve customers across Asia. Below is a selection of the principals we represent.\n## Equipment makers we represent\nSearch by name or technology, or filter by area. For availability in your country, please ask our team .\n### EV Group (EVG)\nWafer bonding, lithography & nanoimprint\nLong-standing principal. In 2023, EVG and Dymek formed the joint venture EV Group Malaysia Dymek Sdn. Bhd. for installation, technical service, spare parts and process support.\n### 4D Technology\nDynamic interferometry & 3D surface metrology\nDynamic interferometers and optical surface metrology, including PhaseCam, AccuFiz, NanoCam and InSpec.\n### 3D-Micromac\nLaser micromachining & laser sample preparation\nLaser micromachining and laser-based sample preparation systems. 3D-Micromac appointed Dymek as a trade agency in December 2025.\n### Integral Solutions International (ISI)\nMRAM, magnetic sensor & data-storage test\nTest systems for MRAM, magnetic sensors and data-storage devices, with sales and technical support through Dymek.\n### CVD Equipment Corporation\nCVD & thermal process equipment\nChemical vapour deposition and thermal process equipment, with sales and technical support through Dymek.\n### KLA (formerly Filmetrics & MicroSense)\nThin-film thickness, capacitive & magnetic metrology\nFilmetrics thin-film measurement systems (such as the F20, F40 and F54) and MicroSense capacitive sensing, wafer geometry and magnetic metrology, both now part of KLA and offered through Dymek.\n### yieldWerx\nYield management & manufacturing analytics\nyieldWerx and Dymek announced a strategic partnership to support semiconductor yield management.\n### Opto-Alignment\nLens centration, optical alignment & assembly\nSystems for centring, aligning and bonding lenses and optical assemblies, used by precision optics, camera-module and photonics manufacturers.\n### Thermo Fisher Scientific (formerly FEI)\nElectron microscopy & failure analysis\nSEM, TEM and DualBeam (FIB-SEM) systems, formerly FEI, for materials characterisation, sample preparation and semiconductor failure analysis.\n### ThetaMetrisis\nThin-film thickness & optical properties\nWhite-light reflectance spectroscopy tools for fast, non-contact measurement of thin-film thickness and optical constants, from R&D to inline use.\n### Beneq\nAtomic layer deposition (ALD)\nALD equipment for research, pilot and production use, depositing highly uniform, conformal thin films for semiconductors, optics and power devices.\n### Sonix\nScanning acoustic microscopy\nScanning acoustic microscopes that find voids, delamination and cracks inside packages, bonded wafers and assemblies without damaging them.\n### CubeFabs (formerly Nanotronics)\nAI-driven automated optical inspection\nAutomated optical inspection combining advanced microscopy with machine learning to detect and classify defects on wafers and devices.\nNo principals match your search. Please contact us and we will point you in the right direction.\n## Looking for a partner in Asia?\nOur mission includes supporting principals in reaching and serving markets across Asia effectively. We offer sales coverage, application support, installation, service and spare-parts logistics across 8 markets.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: contact.html | Contact Dymek | Offices in Hong Kong, China, Taiwan, Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand, Vietnam & Philippines\n# Get in Touch\nTell us what you need and our team will get back to you.\nDymek operates across Asia with offices in Hong Kong (head office), China, Taiwan, Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand, Vietnam and the Philippines.\n## Find your nearest Dymek office\nSelect a marker or an office to see its address and phone number.\n### Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR Head office\nHead office\n### Beijing, China\n### Shanghai, China\n### Dongguan, China\n### Hsinchu, Taiwan\n### Penang, Malaysia\n### Singapore, Singapore\n### Pathum Thani, Thailand\n### Hanoi, Vietnam\n### Manila (Muntinlupa), Philippines\n### Prefer to explore first?\nBrowse our industries to see which technologies match your application.\n\n=== PAGE: careers.html | Careers at Dymek | Engineering, Sales & Operations Jobs in Asia\n# Careers at Dymek: Engineering and Business Roles Across Asia\nWork with advanced technologies that shape semiconductors, optics, aerospace and more. Join a regional team that values practical skills, clear communication and long-term relationships.\n## Why people build careers with us\n### Work with leading technology\nGet hands-on with equipment from leading manufacturers in Europe, the USA, Japan and South Korea.\n### Real problems, real impact\nHelp fabs, labs and factories solve measurement and process challenges that matter to their output.\n### A regional network\nCollaborate with colleagues across 10 offices in 8 markets, and with partners worldwide.\n### Learn and grow\nBuild technical depth through training, partner know-how and experienced colleagues.\n## Where you could fit\n### Application & field service engineering\nInstall, commission and support advanced equipment at customer sites, and help customers get the best results.\n### Sales & business development\nUnderstand customer needs and connect them with the right technology and partners.\n### Operations & administration\nKeep logistics, finance, import and office operations running smoothly across the region.\n## Current openings\nDon’t see the right role? Send a general application. We keep strong profiles on file for future openings.\nStrategic projects & operations\n### Strategic Projects & Operations Manager\nKuala Lumpur, Malaysia\nFull-time\nPrimarily remote\n### View full job description\nReports to: Managing Director SEA · Travel: approx. 20–30% of working time, typically trips of 3–7 days, within Asia and further abroad · Working arrangement: primarily remote, with occasional in-person meetings\nWe are seeking a capable, commercially minded and personable Strategic Projects & Operations Manager to work directly with Dymek's senior management. You will represent Dymek at international exhibitions, customer meetings and, over time, visits to our equipment suppliers, while also taking on a broad range of business and operational projects across Asia and beyond.\nA central part of this role is acting as an extension of senior management. You will begin by attending exhibitions and meetings alongside the Managing Director, learning Dymek's technologies, customers, suppliers and way of doing business. As your knowledge and confidence grow, you will increasingly represent Dymek on your own: presenting our message to customers, building relationships across the industry, and reporting back clearly on what you see and hear.\nAlongside this, the role is hands-on. You will work across commercial, operational and corporate matters, some of which may initially be unfamiliar or loosely defined. Rather than waiting for assigned tasks, you will identify what needs to be done, organize the work, and move matters forward independently, taking on the detailed work yourself where needed.\nWe are looking for someone with sound business judgment, genuine curiosity about technology, and the confidence to represent a company professionally in front of customers and partners. Strong communication, conscientiousness, and the judgment to know when to act independently and when to involve management are central to success.\n- Initially: Attend international exhibitions and conferences together with the Managing Director, learning Dymek's equipment suppliers, technologies, customers, and how Dymek presents itself.\n- Over time: Attend exhibitions independently or as part of the Dymek team, and represent Dymek at customer meetings, in person and virtually, alongside our local sales engineers.\n- Longer term: Visit Dymek's principals (equipment suppliers) on behalf of the company and take on a growing role in managing these relationships.\n- Attend international exhibitions and conferences on behalf of Dymek, initially alongside the Managing Director and later independently or as part of the Dymek team.\n- Prepare for each exhibition by identifying the customers, principals and prospective suppliers to meet, and arranging meetings in advance.\n- At exhibitions, engage with existing and potential customers and principals, identify new technologies and potential new equipment suppliers, and gather market and competitor information.\n- After each event, prepare a concise report covering key contacts, leads, market insights and recommended next steps, and make sure leads are passed to the relevant sales engineers for follow-up.\n- Join customer meetings, in person and virtually, with Dymek's local sales engineers, presenting Dymek's company, capabilities and key messages as agreed with the Managing Director.\n- Report back promptly after customer meetings on what was discussed, customer requirements and concerns, opportunities identified, and agreed follow-up actions.\n- Over time, visit Dymek's principals to represent the company, discuss business performance and plans, and strengthen working relationships.\n- Take ownership of business and operational projects, from initial investigation and planning through to implementation and completion.\n- Work with senior management to identify, organize and prioritize matters requiring attention, including issues that do not fall neatly within an existing business function.\n- Investigate unfamiliar or ambiguous problems, gather relevant information, identify possible solutions, and recommend practical courses of action.\n- Coordinate colleagues, external parties and other resources as necessary, anticipating obstacles and following through on outstanding matters to keep projects moving.\n- Prepare clear, concise updates and recommendations, distinguishing matters you can resolve yourself from those requiring management decisions.\n- Support the evaluation and execution of commercial opportunities, supplier relationships, and business arrangements.\n- Analyze commercial transactions and proposals, including supplier quotations, customer pricing, purchase orders, costs, gross margins, payment terms, and other relevant considerations.\n- Support business planning, budgeting, financial analysis, and management reporting, including gathering and interpreting information from Dymek's various offices.\n- Conduct commercial and market research to support the evaluation of new equipment suppliers, business partnerships, technologies, and geographic opportunities.\n- Review business processes and identify opportunities to improve coordination, efficiency, documentation, and operational effectiveness.\n- Support the establishment and development of overseas offices, subsidiaries, joint ventures, and other business operations.\n- Coordinate with accountants, legal advisers, banks, corporate secretaries, government authorities, and other professional service providers, and assist with commercial agreements, corporate documentation, and compliance matters, working with specialists where necessary.\n- Support recruitment, compensation reviews, employment arrangements, organizational changes, and other HR-related projects in coordination with local management and advisers.\n- Assist with special projects and management initiatives as business needs evolve, including matters outside the usual scope of day-to-day operations.\n- Work closely with Dymek's colleagues across different countries, helping to coordinate activities, share information, and follow through on agreed actions.\n- Build effective working relationships with business partners, professional advisers, and other external parties, communicating clearly and diplomatically with people from different countries, cultures, and professional backgrounds.\n- Travel regularly within Asia and further abroad for exhibitions, customer meetings, principal visits, and ongoing projects.\n- Make effective use of digital tools, including AI assistants, to speed up research, analysis, and documentation, while checking accuracy, recognizing limitations, and maintaining appropriate confidentiality.\n- Maintain organized project records and follow-up processes, and introduce practical improvements that reduce unnecessary manual work.\n- Bachelor's degree in Engineering, Science, Business Administration, Finance, Economics, or a related field, or equivalent practical experience.\n- Typically 6+ years of experience in business development, technical sales, business operations, commercial management, project management, or related roles.\n- Practical experience in a commercial B2B environment, preferably involving distribution, trading, manufacturing, engineering, or technical products and services. Exposure to semiconductor, optical, metrology, or scientific equipment is an advantage.\n- Confident presenter, comfortable presenting a company and its offering to customers, engineers, and senior executives, both in person and online.\n- Technical aptitude, with the ability to quickly understand technical products and applications and explain them clearly to others.\n- Professional presence and strong networking skills, with the ability to build rapport quickly with new contacts at exhibitions, meetings, and industry events.\n- Sound understanding of commercial transactions, including quotations, purchase orders, supplier costs, customer pricing, margins, and payment arrangements.\n- Strong analytical ability and financial literacy, with the capacity to interpret business information and identify important commercial considerations.\n- Demonstrated initiative and resourcefulness, with a track record of independently investigating problems and seeing work through to completion.\n- Excellent written and spoken English, including the ability to write clear, concise reports on meetings and events.\n- Conversational proficiency in Mandarin and/or Cantonese is strongly preferred, given Dymek's business activities across Greater China and the wider Asia region. Full professional fluency is not required.\n- Strong interpersonal skills and cultural awareness, with the ability to work effectively with colleagues, customers, principals, and external advisers across different countries.\n- Conscientious and dependable, with strong attention to detail, personal accountability, and sound judgment, including knowing when management involvement or specialist advice is required.\n- Proficient with Microsoft Office, particularly Excel, Word, and PowerPoint. Experience using AI tools such as ChatGPT or Claude productively in a professional environment would be an advantage.\n- Willing and able to travel internationally on a regular basis, including multi-day exhibitions and trips beyond Southeast Asia.\n- Direct exposure to senior management and a broad view of an established international business.\n- The opportunity to attend leading international exhibitions and meet world-class technology suppliers and customers across the semiconductor, optics, and advanced manufacturing industries.\n- Benefits including annual leave, medical coverage, EPF/SOCSO contributions, and AWS.\n- Flexible, primarily remote working arrangement.\n- Room to grow the role and take on greater responsibility as Dymek's business expands.\nFounded in 1989, Dymek is a trusted distributor of advanced manufacturing and metrology equipment serving leading players in the semiconductor, optics, biotech, aerospace, and R&D sectors. With headquarters in Hong Kong and offices across Asia, we connect customers with world-class technology suppliers from Europe, the US, and Asia Pacific. Our team supports customers through the full sales journey, combining local insight with technical understanding.\nApplication & Field Service Engineering\n### Service & Applications Engineer\nPathum Thani, Thailand\nFull-time\n### View full job description\nWe are seeking a technically minded and dependable Service & Applications Engineer to join our Thailand regional office in Pathum Thani. In this role, you will install, maintain and support the advanced manufacturing and metrology equipment we supply to semiconductor, optics, electronics and data storage manufacturers across Thailand, while working closely with our sales team to help customers choose and get the most out of the right tools. It is well suited to an engineer early in their career who is eager to learn; you will receive structured product training from our principals in Europe, the US and Asia, along with on-the-job guidance from senior engineers across our regional offices.\n- Installing, commissioning and qualifying new equipment at customer sites in line with supplier procedures and customer acceptance criteria\n- Performing scheduled preventive maintenance, calibration and system upgrades to keep installed tools running to specification\n- Diagnosing and resolving hardware, software and process issues on site or remotely, escalating to suppliers with clear technical reports when needed\n- Responding promptly to service calls and equipment breakdowns to minimise customer downtime\n- Managing spare parts requirements, including identifying parts, raising requests and tracking orders through to delivery\n- Preparing service reports, installation records and field documentation after every visit\n- Following customer cleanroom, safety and site access requirements at all times\n- Supporting the sales team with technical input during customer meetings, product presentations and equipment evaluations\n- Preparing and running equipment demonstrations, sample measurements and application trials for prospective customers\n- Understanding customer process requirements and recommending suitable equipment configurations and options\n- Providing technical input for quotations, specifications and tender documents\n- Delivering operator and basic maintenance training to customer engineers and technicians\n- Identifying opportunities for upgrades, service contracts and additional equipment within the installed base\n- Attending product and service training provided by our principals, locally or overseas, and building expertise across our product portfolio\n- Acting as the local technical point of contact between customers and supplier engineers, including coordinating visits by overseas specialists\n- Keeping suppliers informed on installed base status, recurring issues and customer feedback\n- Sharing knowledge with engineers in other Dymek offices and contributing to internal technical documentation\n- Maintaining accurate records of the installed base, service history, warranties and service contracts\n- Coordinating with the office team on the import of tools and parts, customs documentation and delivery schedules\n- Looking after demo equipment, tools and test kits, ensuring they are calibrated and ready for use\n- Submitting service reports, expense claims and travel plans on time\n- Education: Bachelor’s degree in Electrical, Electronic, Mechanical, Mechatronics, Physics, Materials Engineering or a related field\n- Experience: fresh graduates are welcome to apply; 1 to 3 years of experience in field service, equipment maintenance or applications within the semiconductor, optics, electronics or data storage industry is a plus\n- Technical knowledge: solid grounding in electrical and mechanical systems, with the ability to read schematics, drawings and technical manuals. Exposure to metrology, optical inspection, vacuum or wafer processing equipment is an advantage\n- Technical skills: comfortable with Microsoft Office and basic PC and network troubleshooting; familiarity with equipment control software, data analysis or scripting is a bonus\n- Key attributes: a methodical and safety-conscious approach to troubleshooting; the ability to stay calm and organised under production pressure; and a proactive, adaptable and teachable attitude\n- Communication: fluent in Thai with good written and spoken English. Able to explain technical issues clearly to both engineers and non-technical customers\n- Mobility: willing to travel frequently to customer sites across Thailand, particularly the industrial estates around Bangkok, Pathum Thani, Ayutthaya and the Eastern Seaboard, with occasional overseas travel for training. A valid Thai driving licence is preferred\nFounded in 1989, Dymek is a trusted distributor of advanced manufacturing and metrology equipment serving leading players in the semiconductor, optics, aerospace and solar sectors. With headquarters in Hong Kong and offices across Asia, we connect customers with world-class technology suppliers from Europe, the US and Asia Pacific. Our team supports customers through the full sales journey, combining local insight with deep technical understanding. Our Thailand office in Pathum Thani supports manufacturers across the country’s key industrial regions.\nApplication & Field Service Engineering\n### Service Manager\nSingapore\nFull-time\n### View full job description\nWe are seeking a capable, hands-on Service Manager to lead the installation, servicing and technical support of the metrology and process equipment Dymek supplies to customers in the semiconductor, optics, biotech, aerospace and R&D sectors. Based in Singapore, you will hold primary responsibility for service delivery in the local market while contributing your expertise to projects across the wider Asia region. This is very much a hands-on role: while you will coordinate and mentor a small team of field service engineers, you are expected to remain on the tools yourself. You will also be the face of Dymek and its principals in front of customers, communicating clearly how an issue has been resolved and building lasting confidence among customers and suppliers.\n- Install, commission, service and repair metrology and process equipment at customer sites, working hands-on alongside the field service team\n- Diagnose and troubleshoot faults across a broad range of equipment, including tools you may not have encountered before, by reasoning from first principles, technical manuals and supplier documentation\n- Provide on-site and remote technical support to customers, ensuring equipment is restored to full working condition with minimal disruption\n- Contribute hands-on expertise to installation and service projects across the Asia region as required, involving regular regional travel\n- Coordinate and schedule the field service team’s workload, allocating jobs based on skills, location and priority\n- Mentor and develop junior engineers, sharing diagnostic methods and building the team’s technical capability over time\n- Serve as the senior point of escalation for complex or sensitive technical issues\n- Act as the primary technical liaison with customers, communicating clearly how issues have been diagnosed and resolved\n- De-escalate difficult situations and manage customer expectations with professionalism and composure\n- Build and sustain trust with customers and suppliers that Dymek consistently delivers reliable engineering solutions\n- Liaise with equipment suppliers (principals) on technical matters, warranty claims and escalations\n- Identify and follow up on opportunities for service contract renewals\n- Spot and flag opportunities for spare parts sales, upgrades and additional services that benefit the customer\n- Support the sales team with technical input during the pre-sales and quotation process where relevant\n- Prepare clear, accurate service reports, installation records and maintenance documentation for every job\n- Maintain organised records of service history, customer communications and technical findings\n- Manage and track spare parts inventory, ensuring critical parts are available to support timely service\n- Diploma or degree in Mechanical, Electrical, Mechatronics, Electronics Engineering or a related technical field\n- Proven field service experience with precision instruments, capital equipment or technical systems; a background in metrology, semiconductor, optics or related industries is an advantage but not essential\n- Strong, demonstrable ability to troubleshoot unfamiliar equipment independently by interpreting technical manuals, schematics and engineering documentation\n- Comfortable taking ownership of technical issues and working independently, while knowing when to seek support from colleagues or equipment suppliers\n- Some experience mentoring, guiding or coordinating junior engineers or technical staff would be an advantage\n- Excellent communication skills, with the ability to explain technical issues clearly, handle challenging situations professionally and inspire customer and supplier confidence\n- Conscientious and disciplined about documentation\n- Willingness to travel regularly within Singapore and across the Asia region as projects require\nFounded in 1989, Dymek is a trusted distributor of advanced manufacturing and metrology equipment serving leading players in the semiconductor, optics, aerospace and solar sectors. With headquarters in Hong Kong and offices across Asia, we connect customers with world-class technology suppliers from Europe, the US and Asia Pacific. Our team supports customers through the full sales journey, combining local insight with deep technical understanding.\nOperations & Administration\n### Accounts & Office Operations Administrator\nMalaysia\nFull-time\n### View full job description\nWe are seeking a meticulous and versatile Accounts & Office Operations Administrator to help keep our office running smoothly day to day. In this role, you will handle the full accounting cycle for the organisation while assisting across sales administration, logistics, payroll and general office functions. It is well suited to someone early in their career who is eager to learn; you will develop full-set accounting and supporting administrative functions on the job, with guidance from the company secretary, auditors and the wider team. We value reliability, discretion and a proactive, teachable mindset over rigid role boundaries.\n- Progressively taking on the full accounting cycle, with guidance and training\n- Recording and verifying supplier invoices, matching them against Purchase Orders (PO) and Delivery Orders (DO)\n- Processing supplier payments (AP) and issuing customer invoices while monitoring the receivables ledger for timely collections (AR)\n- Handling monthly bank reconciliations, performing journal entries and managing petty cash\n- Preparing the Profit & Loss Account, Balance Sheet and Trial Balance\n- Managing monthly staff salary processing alongside statutory deductions and submissions (EPF, SOCSO and PCB/LHDN)\n- Maintaining proper documentation for all financial records\n- Liaising directly with banks, the company secretary, auditors and tax agents\n- Providing backend support for sales personnel to ensure seamless commercial operations\n- Preparing and formatting customer quotations\n- Processing sales orders and invoices\n- Registering and maintaining Dymek’s status as an approved vendor in customer procurement systems\n- Managing end-to-end logistics and shipment administration, coordinating inbound and outbound shipments and tracking delivery status\n- Preparing shipping documentation such as commercial invoices, packing lists, delivery notes and airway bills\n- Maintaining proactive communication with suppliers, customers and freight forwarders to confirm on-time deliveries\n- Compiling operational reports and handling shipment claims for damaged or delayed goods\n- Overseeing general office upkeep, including office utilities, supplies and cleaning services\n- Maintaining organised physical and digital filing systems\n- Handling banking or corporate errands\n- Assisting colleagues and field engineers with expense claims, travel requirements and compensation\n- Supporting basic HR activities, including organising office events and team-building initiatives\n- Education: Diploma or degree in Accounting, Finance, Business Administration or a related field\n- Experience: fresh graduates are highly welcome to apply; up to 2 years of relevant experience is a plus but not required\n- Core knowledge: basic understanding of accounting principles, with a genuine willingness to be trained on full-set accounts, payroll and logistics. Awareness of Malaysian statutory requirements (EPF, SOCSO, PCB/LHDN) is an advantage\n- Technical skills: comfortable with Microsoft Office, particularly Excel; exposure to accounting software is a bonus\n- Key attributes: a sharp eye for precision; strong organisational skills to handle varied tasks simultaneously; and a proactive, adaptable and teachable attitude\n- Communication: good communication skills and the strict discretion required to handle confidential financial and staff information\nFounded in 1989, Dymek is a trusted distributor of advanced manufacturing and metrology equipment serving leading players in the semiconductor, optics, aerospace and solar sectors. With headquarters in Hong Kong and offices across Asia, we connect customers with world-class technology suppliers from Europe, the US and Asia Pacific. Our team supports customers through the full sales journey, combining local insight with deep technical understanding.\n## What to expect\n- Apply Send your details and CV through the form below.\n- First conversation A short call to understand your background and goals.\n- Interview Meet the team and discuss practical or technical scenarios.\n- Offer We confirm the role, location and start date with you.\n## Send your application\nTell us about yourself and the kind of role you’re looking for. You can apply for an open role or send a general application.\n\n=== PAGE: blog.html | Insights: Semiconductor, Advanced Packaging, Optics & Metrology Guides | Dymek\n# Insights on Semiconductor, Optics & Metrology\nClear, practical guides to the processes, measurements and trends that underpin advanced manufacturing in Asia.\nFeatured · Advanced Packaging\n## What Is Hybrid Bonding? Wafer-to-Wafer vs Die-to-Wafer Explained\nHybrid bonding joins two chips through copper pads and dielectric simultaneously, without solder bumps. This guide explains how it works, where it is used and what is required to achieve good yield.\nAdvanced Packaging\n### What Is Hybrid Bonding? Wafer-to-Wafer vs Die-to-Wafer Explained\nHybrid bonding joins two chips through copper pads and dielectric simultaneously, without solder bumps. This guide explains how it works, where it is used and what is required to achieve good yield.\nPhotonics\n### Co-Packaged Optics (CPO) Explained: Why AI Data Centers Are Moving Optics onto the Package\nNvidia and Broadcom are now shipping co-packaged optics switches. This article explains what CPO is, why it reduces power consumption and what makes it challenging to manufacture.\nFailure Analysis\n### How Laser Sample Preparation Speeds Up Semiconductor Failure Analysis\nFailure analysis is frequently held up by sample preparation. Laser-based preparation removes bulk material rapidly, so that FIB time can be reserved for the final, precise step.\nMetrology\n### Thin Film Thickness Measurement: Reflectometry vs Ellipsometry vs Interferometry\nThree optical methods dominate thin-film measurement. This guide sets out what each one measures, where it performs best and how to choose between them.\nPower Semiconductors\n### SiC and GaN Power Device Manufacturing: Key Process, Test and Inspection Challenges\nWide-bandgap power devices are at the heart of EV inverters and fast chargers. Their materials make every process step more demanding. This article outlines where the principal challenges lie.\nIndustry Insight\n### Southeast Asia's Semiconductor Growth: What It Means for Equipment Buyers\nSoutheast Asia is progressing from assembly towards advanced packaging, design and specialty fabs. This article considers what that shift means when selecting equipment and support.\nNo articles match. Try another topic.\n## Have a topic you want us to cover?\nTell us what you’re working on. We write about the questions engineers and buyers actually ask us.\n\n=== PAGE: advanced-packaging-assembly.html | Advanced Packaging Equipment: Hybrid Bonding, Chiplets, HBM & 2.5D/3D | Dymek\n# Advanced Packaging & Assembly Equipment\nAdvanced packaging is now a critical part of semiconductor performance. It brings multiple dies, materials and functions together in ever more compact systems. Dymek provides process and assembly technologies for wafer-level packaging, heterogeneous integration, 2.5D/3D integration and next-generation device architectures.\nOur solutions support process development through high-volume manufacturing for chiplets, high-bandwidth memory (HBM), fan-out packages, system-in-package (SiP), optical modules and other advanced assemblies.\n### Not sure which technology fits?\nSend us your material, feature size and target spec. We’ll suggest a method and, where possible, a feasibility test.\n## Wafer-to-Wafer & Die-to-Wafer Bonding\nWe support fusion, direct and hybrid bonding for wafer-to-wafer and die-to-wafer integration. These technologies create fine-pitch electrical and mechanical connections between device layers. They are increasingly important for 3D integration, image sensors, logic-memory integration, chiplets and HBM.\nA complete process flow can include surface preparation, cleaning, activation, alignment, bonding and post-bond metrology.\n## Temporary Bonding, Debonding & Thin-Wafer Handling\nThin wafers need reliable mechanical support while they are thinned, processed and transferred. We provide temporary bonding and debonding, carrier handling and layer-release processes for advanced packaging, power devices and other thin-substrate applications.\nThese processes support backside processing, redistribution layers (RDL), fan-out packaging and other workflows where wafer fragility and warpage must be carefully controlled.\n## Precision Die Placement & Thermocompression\nAdvanced packages need high-accuracy placement of known-good die onto wafers, interposers or substrates. We support precision die bonding, flip-chip assembly, thermocompression bonding and related placement processes for fine-pitch interconnects and heterogeneous devices.\nApplications range from R&D and low-volume assembly to production systems for multi-die packages and high-density integration.\n## 2.5D, 3D, Chiplets & HBM\nHigh-performance computing and AI are speeding up the adoption of chiplet architectures, silicon interposers and vertically stacked memory. We support the bonding, alignment, thin-wafer processing and inspection steps these structures require.\nThe same capabilities apply to other high-density packages that combine logic, memory, sensors, RF, photonics or specialty devices in one package.\n## Optical & Photonic Packaging\nAs optical and electronic functions converge, packaging needs precise alignment between photonic devices, lenses, fiber arrays and electronic components. We support optical component placement, fiber-array alignment, lens alignment and related assembly for optical modules, co-packaged optics and optical I/O.\nThese applications combine semiconductor packaging methods with active or passive optical alignment and high-precision motion.\nHigh-precision die placement and optical coupling also support specialty optoelectronic and sensor packages, such as:\n- LiDAR emitters and receivers\n- Commercial laser modules and infrared devices\n- MEMS and CMOS image sensors\n- RF and hybrid devices\nDepending on the application, assembly can combine eutectic, sintering, thermocompression, adhesive or other bonding methods with automated alignment and handling.\n## Package Inspection & Process Control\nAdvanced packages contain buried interfaces that visual inspection cannot always reach. We provide X-ray and CT inspection, scanning acoustic microscopy, automated optical inspection and other metrology techniques. They detect voids, delamination, die cracks, underfill defects, Cu-pillar issues, solder defects and other package abnormalities.\nInspection data supports process development, incoming quality control, production monitoring and failure analysis. For targeted root-cause work, laser-based sample preparation can quickly produce large-area package cross-sections. It can also expose regions of interest in interposers, TSV/RDL structures, stacked dies and hybrid-bonded interfaces before microscopy or ion-beam analysis.\nNon-contact 3D surface metrology adds measurement of package and substrate topography, warpage, transparent-layer thickness and fine feature geometry. This helps process development for advanced substrates, redistribution structures and other package elements where dimensional variation affects alignment or bonding.\n## From Assembly Development to Production\nFor specialized packages, we combine custom fixtures, precision motion, handling, machine vision and process software around the core equipment. Projects can move from feasibility trials and process integration into semi- or fully automated production.\n## Related insights\nAdvanced Packaging\n### What Is Hybrid Bonding? Wafer-to-Wafer vs Die-to-Wafer Explained\nHybrid bonding joins two chips through copper pads and dielectric at the same time, with no solder bumps. Here is how it works, where it is used and what it takes to get yield.\n## Explore connected areas\nFront-end\n### Wafer Processing & Device Fabrication\nLithography, wafer bonding, plasma etch and deposition, furnaces, dicing, laser annealing, CMP and thin-film metrology for silicon, MEMS and more.\nAI & HPC\n### AI Computing & Data Center Hardware\nHybrid bonding for HBM and chiplets, co-packaged optics and optical I/O, advanced package inspection, failure analysis and more.\nYield & quality\n### Metrology, Inspection & Failure Analysis\nThin-film metrology, wafer AOI, X-ray/CT, scanning acoustic microscopy, probing, SEM/TEM and more.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: aerospace.html | Aerospace Inspection & Metrology Equipment for Manufacturing and MRO | Dymek\n# Aerospace Manufacturing & MRO Inspection Equipment\nAerospace manufacturing and maintenance need accurate, repeatable inspection of critical components, surfaces and materials. Dymek provides metrology, inspection and materials-characterization equipment for new-part manufacturing and for maintenance, repair and overhaul (MRO).\nOur technologies support defect inspection, dimensional measurement, surface characterization, materials testing, failure analysis and automated quality control. We can also build inspection equipment into custom systems designed around your part and process.\n## Portable & In-Situ Defect Inspection\nPortable 3D inspection systems let engineers measure defects directly on large or hard-to-move parts. There is no need to send them to a metrology lab. Non-contact systems measure scratches, pits, nicks, dents, corrosion, edge breaks, radii and other surface features.\nTypical applications include turbine blades, engine components, machined parts and hard-to-reach surfaces. Quantitative 3D measurement reduces reliance on visual inspection and manual gauges. Engineers make faster, more consistent repair, rework and acceptance decisions.\n## Automated 3D Inspection & Dimensional Metrology\nWe support automated 3D inspection of aerospace components using non-contact optical measurement, machine vision, robotics and precision motion. These systems check dimensional features, surface geometry and production defects right on the factory floor.\nApplications include complex machined components, composite structures, castings and large assemblies where coordinate measurement or manual inspection is slow or hard to automate.\n## Materials, Coatings & Tribology\nWe provide laboratory equipment for testing aerospace materials, coatings and lubricants. It measures friction, wear, fretting, adhesion, hardness, surface roughness and coating performance.\nTests can run under controlled conditions such as high or cryogenic temperature, vacuum, humidity and high mechanical loads. This supports development and qualification of engine components, bearings, gears, actuators, composites, lubricants and aerospace coatings.\n## Microscopy & Failure Analysis\nWhen defects need closer investigation, electron microscopy and related techniques support materials analysis, fracture investigation, contamination analysis and root-cause studies.\nThese tools complement dimensional and surface inspection with detail on microstructure, interfaces and failure mechanisms.\n## Aerospace & Defense Optical Assembly\nAerospace, space and defense systems can contain large-aperture or deep multi-element optical assemblies. Mechanical references alone often cannot reach the required optical-axis accuracy. We support non-contact active alignment of lenses and optical surfaces during assembly, including large imaging systems, laser collimators, telescope-related assemblies and other precision optical modules.\nFor infrared and electro-optical systems, we also support specialized thin-film coatings for thermal imaging, night vision and other IR optics, including protective and low-loss coatings for harsh environments.\n## Automated Inspection & Custom Metrology Systems\nWe develop semi- or fully automated inspection systems around your component and production process. We integrate metrology equipment with robots, precision motion, machine vision, part handling and production software.\nCustom fixtures and part-handling systems position components accurately and repeatably during inspection. We can also deliver:\n- Mechanical design and safety enclosures\n- Control systems and measurement recipes\n- Automated data collection and pass/fail reporting\n- Connection to factory databases and existing quality systems\n## From Feasibility to Production\nWhen your application needs custom hardware, we support the design and contract manufacturing of fixtures, tooling, mechanical assemblies and other system components. We manage projects from feasibility testing and measurement development through system design, fabrication, integration, installation and production support.\n### Not sure which technology fits?\nSend us your material, feature size and target spec. We’ll suggest a method and, where possible, a feasibility test.\n## Explore connected areas\nIndustrial\n### Industrial & Precision Manufacturing\nAutomated 3D inspection beyond CMM, portable surface gauges, machine vision AOI, tribology and coating testing, laser micro-drilling and more.\nComponents\n### Precision Optics & Optical Manufacturing\nInterferometers, non-contact 3D surface metrology, thin-film measurement, lens centration and active alignment and more.\nService\n### Custom System Integration\nSome applications don't fit a standard tool. We build semi- or fully automated systems around your part and process, combining proven instruments with robotics, motion, machine vision, fixtures and software.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: ai-computing-data-center-hardware.html | AI Hardware Manufacturing: HBM, Chiplets, Co-Packaged Optics & Inspection | Dymek\n# AI Computing & Data Center Hardware Manufacturing\nAI and high-performance computing are rapidly changing semiconductor architecture, memory bandwidth, optical interconnects and thermal management. Dymek provides process, packaging, inspection and photonics technologies for the hardware supply chain behind AI accelerators and next-generation data centers.\nOur focus is on making and integrating the chips, packages, optical interfaces and advanced components inside high-performance computing systems. We do not supply facility-level data center infrastructure.\n### Not sure which technology fits?\nSend us your material, feature size and target spec. We’ll suggest a method and, where possible, a feasibility test.\n## AI Accelerators, HBM & Advanced Packaging\nAI processors increasingly rely on high-bandwidth memory, chiplets, advanced substrates and 2.5D/3D integration. We support wafer-to-wafer and die-to-wafer bonding, hybrid bonding, thin-wafer handling, precision die placement and related heterogeneous-integration processes.\nThese technologies are used in HBM stacks, logic-memory integration, chiplet architectures, fan-out packaging and other high-density packages. They increase bandwidth while shortening interconnects and lowering power use.\n## Co-Packaged Optics & High-Speed Optical Interconnects\nElectrical interconnects are becoming a bottleneck, so optical links are moving closer to switches, processors and accelerators. We support silicon photonics, optical I/O and co-packaged optics through wafer bonding, nanoimprint, photonic device processing, precision optical alignment and optical/electrical device testing.\nApplications include coupling structures, gratings, lenses, fiber-array alignment, photonic dies and other elements that connect high-speed optical networks directly to advanced electronic packages.\n## Inspection, Yield & Failure Analysis\nAI hardware combines expensive dies, dense interconnects and multiple stacked layers. Early defect detection and fast failure analysis matter more than ever. We provide automated optical inspection, X-ray and CT, scanning acoustic microscopy, thin-film metrology, electron microscopy and other methods for wafers and advanced packages.\nLaser-based sample preparation speeds up access to regions of interest in complex packages before SEM, FIB or TEM analysis. Emerging workflows target TSVs, redistribution layers, interposers and buried bonding interfaces in 2.5D/3D packages.\n## Manufacturing Data & Yield Analytics\nAI accelerators, HBM and photonic modules combine costly dies and process steps, so yield learning and traceability are increasingly important. We provide software for process and yield analytics, defect management, statistical process control, wafer and assembly mapping, automated reporting and data correlation across fabrication, packaging and test.\nCustom data integrations connect metrology, inspection and test results with your existing manufacturing systems. Engineers can spot process drift, isolate yield loss and find root causes faster.\n## Optoelectronic Device Characterization\nOptical interconnects rely on lasers, VCSELs, photodiodes and other optoelectronic devices that need electrical and optical testing. We support wafer- and chip-level probing, optical power and wavelength measurement, beam characterization and related evaluation during development and production.\n## Thermal Management & Emerging Cooling Technologies\nRising power density creates new needs for heat spreading, cooling and fluid delivery close to high-performance devices. We support precision micromachining and custom process development for advanced thermal-management structures.\nEmerging approaches include internal channels, microfluidic structures and other features in glass or related materials. For new applications, we help evaluate feasibility and develop the fixtures, handling and automation.\n## From Feasibility to Production\nAI hardware architectures change quickly and often need non-standard process combinations. We support feasibility work and integration of process tools, metrology, alignment, fixtures and automation as new packaging, photonic and thermal concepts move toward repeatable production.\n## Related insights\nAdvanced Packaging\n### What Is Hybrid Bonding? Wafer-to-Wafer vs Die-to-Wafer Explained\nHybrid bonding joins two chips through copper pads and dielectric at the same time, with no solder bumps. Here is how it works, where it is used and what it takes to get yield.\nPhotonics\n### Co-Packaged Optics (CPO) Explained: Why AI Data Centers Are Moving Optics onto the Package\nNvidia and Broadcom are now shipping co-packaged optics switches. Here is what CPO is, why it saves power, and what makes it hard to manufacture.\n## Explore connected areas\nBack-end\n### Advanced Packaging & Assembly\nHybrid bonding, wafer-to-wafer and die-to-wafer bonding, temporary bonding/debonding, die placement, thermocompression and more.\nYield & quality\n### Metrology, Inspection & Failure Analysis\nThin-film metrology, wafer AOI, X-ray/CT, scanning acoustic microscopy, probing, SEM/TEM and more.\nPhotonic devices\n### Photonics, Optoelectronics & Optical Interconnects\nPhotonic device fabrication, wafer-level photonic integration, co-packaged optics and optical I/O, active alignment and more.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: ar-vr-wafer-level-optics.html | AR/VR Waveguide & Wafer-Level Optics Manufacturing: Nanoimprint, Lens Stacking | Dymek\n# AR/VR & Wafer-Level Optics Manufacturing\nThis sector covers wafer-scale and replicated manufacture of micro- and nanostructured optical components. Dymek supports AR/VR waveguides, diffractive optical elements, microlens arrays and compact wafer-level optical systems, from early process development to scalable production.\n## Micro- & Nano-Optical Structures\nNanoimprint lithography is a scalable way to replicate micro- and nanoscale optical structures across wafer-size substrates. We support processes for diffractive optical elements, optical waveguides, gratings, microlenses and other structured optical surfaces.\nTechnologies include master fabrication, UV nanoimprint, hot embossing and transfer of optical structures into production substrates. Nanoimprint also scales to large-area panel substrates for displays and photonic structures such as wire-grid polarizers. Applications go beyond AR/VR into sensing, illumination, imaging and other miniaturized optical systems.\n## Wafer-Level Lens Manufacturing & Assembly\nWafer-level manufacturing makes and assembles large numbers of optical elements in parallel. Capabilities include microlens molding, wafer-level replication, precision alignment, UV bonding and aligned stacking of lens and spacer wafers.\nThese processes support wafer-level cameras, 3D sensing, infrared sensing, compact imaging modules and other systems where size, alignment and throughput are critical.\nHigh-precision die attach and optoelectronic assembly also support AR/VR emitters, image sensors, MEMS devices and other compact optical modules that need micron-level placement and reliable bonding.\n## AR/VR Waveguide & High-Index Glass Processing\nAR/VR eyepieces and waveguides often use high-index glasses and other transparent crystalline materials that are hard to process mechanically. Precision laser technologies can separate and process these materials at wafer and component level.\nApplications include singulation of AR waveguides and eyepieces, laser cutting, structuring and other processing of transparent substrates with minimal chipping and mechanical damage.\n## Etch, Deposition & Photonic Processing\nMicro- and nano-optical devices may need semiconductor-style fabrication alongside conventional optical manufacturing. We provide plasma etching, thin-film deposition, surface treatment and related equipment for optical and photonic materials.\nThese support waveguides, gratings, microLED structures and other devices that combine optical function with microfabricated features.\nThin-film coating processes also support:\n- Precision waveguide coatings\n- Hard anti-reflective surfaces\n- Transparent conductive layers and electrochromic structures\n- Controlled transmission or color on smart-glass and wearable-display parts\nFor microLED and related display technologies, laser processes also support high-throughput lift-off and transfer, plus local repair or rework of defective dies and display elements during production.\n## Metrology & Process Control\nMeasurement is needed throughout wafer-level optical manufacturing to control substrates, films and finished optical performance. We support thin-film thickness and optical-property measurement, surface and topography characterization, and interferometric measurement of optical components and systems.\nHigh-speed non-contact 3D metrology inspects microlens arrays, diffractive features and transparent films. Display-oriented measurements quantify photo-spacer geometry, color-filter structures, local defects and other features that cause optical non-uniformity.\nAutomated inspection and machine vision can also be adapted to detect defects and process variation across wafers and optical components.\n## From Process Development to Production\nNew optical designs may combine imprinting, laser processing, bonding, deposition and metrology with no standard equipment setup. We support feasibility trials, custom tooling, handling and inspection, then automate the process as it moves to pilot or volume manufacturing.\n### Not sure which technology fits?\nSend us your material, feature size and target spec. We’ll suggest a method and, where possible, a feasibility test.\n## Explore connected areas\nComponents\n### Precision Optics & Optical Manufacturing\nInterferometers, non-contact 3D surface metrology, thin-film measurement, lens centration and active alignment and more.\nDisplays\n### Displays & Advanced Optoelectronics\nMicroLED and miniLED laser lift-off, transfer and repair, large-area nanoimprint, panel and color-filter metrology and more.\nPhotonic devices\n### Photonics, Optoelectronics & Optical Interconnects\nPhotonic device fabrication, wafer-level photonic integration, co-packaged optics and optical I/O, active alignment and more.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: automotive-e-mobility.html | Automotive & EV Manufacturing Inspection: SiC Power Devices, Batteries, LiDAR | Dymek\n# Automotive & E-Mobility Manufacturing and Inspection\nAutomotive manufacturing now combines mechanical systems with batteries, power electronics, sensors and advanced electronic assemblies. Dymek provides inspection, materials-characterization, semiconductor, optical and automation technologies, from component development and incoming inspection through production quality control and reliability testing.\n## Vehicle & Component Inspection\nAutomated, non-contact inspection combines optical 3D measurement, machine vision and robotics. It handles dimensional control and defect detection on trim, castings, machined or molded parts and other vehicle components.\nPortable or automated surface metrology measures paint and coating texture, scratches, pits, edge features and roughness directly on parts or body surfaces. This links surface preparation with final appearance and component quality.\n## Materials, Coatings & Tribology\nFriction, wear, coatings and surface condition directly affect efficiency, noise, durability and reliability. We provide tribology and surface-characterization equipment for bearings, gears, brakes, lubricants, coatings, electrical contacts and e-drive systems.\nMeasurements include friction, wear, fretting, adhesion, hardness, roughness, film thickness and coating durability under controlled temperature, load and environment. Typical uses:\n- Brake-material screening\n- Lubrication and wear in electrified drivetrains\n- Connector fretting with live electrical-contact monitoring\n- Scratch and mar resistance of paints and exterior coatings\n## EV Batteries & Electronic Assemblies\nElectric vehicles add inspection needs for batteries, power modules and high-density electronic assemblies. We support 2D X-ray, 3D CT and automated X-ray inspection for battery structures, solder joints, die-cast components and electronic modules.\nOptical or automated inspection can be added where surface or dimensional quality must be checked alongside internal features.\n## Power Semiconductors\nSiC and GaN devices are increasingly important in traction inverters, charging systems and other high-voltage vehicle electronics. We provide wafer processing, laser annealing, inspection and failure-analysis technologies for developing and manufacturing wide-bandgap power devices.\nCapabilities include thermal and plasma processing, selective laser annealing, laser trimming, wafer separation and dicing, thin-film metrology, wafer inspection and package analysis.\nWafer-level electrical characterization supports high-current, high-voltage testing of SiC and GaN power devices across controlled temperature ranges. This helps evaluate performance and reliability before packaging.\n### Not sure which technology fits?\nSend us your material, feature size and target spec. We’ll suggest a method and, where possible, a feasibility test.\n## Sensors & MEMS\nVehicles rely on MEMS and magnetic sensors for motion, pressure, steering, throttle, safety and driver-assistance functions. We support wafer bonding, microfabrication, magnetic-sensor processing, inspection and characterization for these devices.\nFor MEMS that need controlled cavity conditions, aligned wafer bonding provides hermetic or high-vacuum encapsulation for inertial sensors, microbolometers and other sensing devices used in automotive and autonomous systems.\nPrecision optical and photonic technologies also support sensing and imaging applications that need optical components or alignment.\n## LiDAR, Cameras & Optical Sensors\nDriver-assistance and autonomous systems depend on LiDAR, cameras, infrared imaging and other optical sensors. We support precision die attach and optoelectronic packaging for emitters, receivers, CMOS image sensors and IR modules. We also support active alignment of lenses, camera objectives, detector planes, apertures and reticles where optical-axis accuracy affects performance.\nSpecialized coatings support:\n- High-performance LiDAR optics\n- Driver-monitoring and front/rear camera optics\n- Night-vision systems, rain and ambient-light sensors\n- Protective sensor covers and smart or electrochromic vehicle glass\n## EMC, Reliability & Failure Analysis\nAs vehicles carry more high-speed electronics and power conversion, electromagnetic compatibility (EMC) and reliability matter more. We support PCB- and IC-level EMC development, X-ray and acoustic inspection, electron microscopy and other failure-analysis techniques for automotive electronics. High-resolution analysis also supports investigation of power-semiconductor metallization and interconnects after electrical and thermal stress testing.\n## From Feasibility to Production\nWe integrate robotics, machine vision, metrology, handling, fixtures and production software around non-standard components or processes. Optional additions include optimization, dashboards, reporting and inspection/test-data integration.\n## Related insights\nPower Semiconductors\n### SiC and GaN Power Device Manufacturing: Key Process, Test and Inspection Challenges\nWide-bandgap power devices power EV inverters and fast chargers. Their materials make every process step harder. Here is where the challenges are.\n## Explore connected areas\nFront-end\n### Wafer Processing & Device Fabrication\nLithography, wafer bonding, plasma etch and deposition, furnaces, dicing, laser annealing, CMP and thin-film metrology for silicon, MEMS and more.\nPhotonic devices\n### Photonics, Optoelectronics & Optical Interconnects\nPhotonic device fabrication, wafer-level photonic integration, co-packaged optics and optical I/O, active alignment and more.\nElectronics\n### Electronics Assembly, Test & Reliability\nSMT and PCB inspection, X-ray and CT for electronics, laser microvia drilling, EMC pre-compliance, RF and sub-THz probing and more.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: calibration-service.html | Equipment Calibration, Field Service & Spare Parts in Asia | Dymek\n# Calibration & Service\nEquipment only creates value while it runs well. Our regional service teams keep your tools accurate, available and supported for their full working life.\n## Service we provide\n- Installation and commissioning of new and relocated systems\n- Calibration and preventive maintenance to keep measurements accurate and repeatable\n- Field service and troubleshooting from engineers based in the region\n- Repair and refurbishment of selected subsystems, including vacuum pumps, RF generators and matching networks\n- Legacy equipment upgrades for mature furnaces and thermal tools\n- Spare parts and obsolescence support , including compatible alternatives\n- Relocation with de-installation, transport coordination and re-installation\n- Operator and engineer training\n## Local support with manufacturer backing\nWe coordinate closely with equipment manufacturers and specialist service partners. In Malaysia, for example, EV Group and Dymek operate the joint venture EV Group Malaysia Dymek Sdn. Bhd., which handles equipment installation, technical service, spare parts and process development support.\n## Keeping mature tools productive\nResearch fabs, MEMS lines, power semiconductor plants and specialty manufacturers often rely on mature tools. We help you decide when refurbishment, subsystem replacement or full replacement makes most sense.\n## Where this service applies\nService\n### Equipment Service & Fab Support\nRepair, refurbishment and replacement of vacuum pumps, RF generators and matching networks, legacy furnace upgrades, relocation and more.\nService\n### Metrology Equipment Sales\nWe connect you with measurement, inspection and process equipment from our principals in Europe, the USA, Japan and South Korea. Our engineers help you choose the right tool for your application, then support it on the ground.\nService\n### Custom System Integration\nSome applications don't fit a standard tool. We build semi- or fully automated systems around your part and process, combining proven instruments with robotics, motion, machine vision, fixtures and software.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: co-packaged-optics-explained.html | Co-Packaged Optics (CPO) Explained: Benefits, Manufacturing Challenges & 2026 Status | Dymek\nPhotonics\n# Co-Packaged Optics (CPO) Explained: Why AI Data Centres Are Moving Optics onto the Package\nNvidia and Broadcom are now shipping co-packaged optics switches. This article explains what CPO is, why it reduces power consumption, and why it is demanding to manufacture.\nAI clusters transfer very large volumes of data between accelerators and switches. Carrying that data over copper to pluggable optical transceivers at the front of a switch consumes considerable power. Co-packaged optics (CPO) shortens this path by placing the optics directly alongside the switch chip.\n## What is co-packaged optics?\nIn a conventional switch, the switch ASIC sends electrical signals across the circuit board to pluggable transceivers, each of which converts those signals into light.\nIn CPO, optical engines are placed in the same package as the switch ASIC . The electrical path is reduced from many centimetres to a few millimetres. This lowers both power per bit and latency, and frees space on the front panel.\n## Where CPO stands in 2026\nA TrendForce report, covered by Electronics360 in July 2026, notes the following:\n- Nvidia and Broadcom have both entered volume production of CPO switches.\n- Broadcom's Bailly CPO switch reduces power consumption by up to 70% compared with conventional pluggable optics, and has been validated with hyperscalers such as Meta.\n- Nvidia's Spectrum-X CPO switch, developed with TSMC's COUPE packaging technology, offers up to 400 Tb/s of switching capacity.\n- TrendForce expects the main volume ramp of the CPO switch market to take place between 2027 and 2028.\nThe same report identifies three supply bottlenecks: optical engines, silicon photonics and advanced packaging . Each of these is, at its core, a manufacturing challenge.\n## Why CPO is difficult to manufacture\n1. Fibre-to-chip coupling. Light must be coupled from a photonic chip into fibres with micron or sub-micron accuracy. Even a small misalignment results in significant optical loss. Active alignment, precision motion, and stable bonding and curing are therefore essential.\n2. Heterogeneous integration. CPO combines electronic dies, photonic dies, lasers and optics within a single package. This calls for die-to-wafer bonding, thin-wafer handling and precise die placement, which is the same toolset used for chiplets and HBM.\n3. Coupling structures. Grating couplers, edge couplers, micro-lenses and beam-shaping elements must be fabricated accurately at wafer scale, frequently by lithography or nanoimprint.\n4. Known-good optical engines. As a CPO package is costly, each optical engine must be tested prior to assembly. This requires wafer- and chip-level optical and electrical probing of optical power, wavelength, beam behaviour and frequency response.\n5. Inspection and failure analysis. Dense packages can conceal internal defects. X-ray, acoustic microscopy and targeted laser cross-sections help to identify root causes quickly.\n## What this means for manufacturers in Asia\nA large share of the world's optical module and advanced packaging capacity is located in Asia. Companies moving from pluggable modules into CPO, optical I/O or silicon photonics require precision alignment, photonic packaging, metrology and test capability, together with engineering support close at hand.\nDymek supports each of these steps through photonic device processing, wafer bonding, nanoimprint, optical alignment and optoelectronic test. Explore our photonics and optical interconnect solutions , or contact us to discuss your CPO roadmap.\n## Sources\n- Electronics360: CPO switch production ramps up by Nvidia and Broadcom (TrendForce), July 2026\n## More insights\nAdvanced Packaging\n### What Is Hybrid Bonding? Wafer-to-Wafer vs Die-to-Wafer Explained\nHybrid bonding joins two chips through copper pads and dielectric at the same time, with no solder bumps. Here is how it works, where it is used and what it takes to get yield.\nFailure Analysis\n### How Laser Sample Preparation Speeds Up Semiconductor Failure Analysis\nFailure analysis often waits on sample preparation. Laser-based preparation removes bulk material fast, so FIB time goes to the final, precise step.\nMetrology\n### Thin Film Thickness Measurement: Reflectometry vs Ellipsometry vs Interferometry\nThree optical methods dominate thin-film measurement. Here is what each one measures, where it shines and how to choose.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: custom-system-integration.html | Custom Metrology & Automation System Integration | Dymek\n# Custom System Integration\nSome applications don't fit a standard tool. We build semi- or fully automated systems around your part and process, combining proven instruments with robotics, motion, machine vision, fixtures and software.\n## What we integrate\n- Metrology and inspection instruments\n- Robots and precision motion systems\n- Machine vision and automated handling\n- Custom fixtures, tooling and safety enclosures\n- Control systems, measurement recipes and pass/fail logic\n- Data collection, reporting and connection to factory databases and quality systems\n## How a project runs\n- Feasibility testing. We test whether the measurement or process works on your real parts.\n- Measurement and process development. We define the method, recipe and acceptance criteria with your engineers.\n- System design. Mechanical design, motion, handling, safety and software are specified around your line.\n- Fabrication and integration. Fixtures, tooling and assemblies are built, including through contract manufacturing, then integrated and tested.\n- Installation. The system is installed and commissioned at your site.\n- Production support. We stay involved with service, recipe updates and improvements.\n## Where it is used\nCustom integration appears across our industries: automated 3D inspection of aerospace and industrial parts, optical assembly cells, advanced packaging assembly, failure-analysis workflows, display repair and photovoltaic laser processing.\n## Where this service applies\nAerospace\n### Aerospace Manufacturing & MRO\nPortable 3D defect inspection for turbine blades, automated dimensional metrology, tribology and coating testing, failure analysis and more.\nIndustrial\n### Industrial & Precision Manufacturing\nAutomated 3D inspection beyond CMM, portable surface gauges, machine vision AOI, tribology and coating testing, laser micro-drilling and more.\nBack-end\n### Advanced Packaging & Assembly\nHybrid bonding, wafer-to-wafer and die-to-wafer bonding, temporary bonding/debonding, die placement, thermocompression and more.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: data-storage-magnetic-devices.html | Data Storage, MRAM & Magnetic Sensor Metrology Equipment | Dymek\n# Data Storage & Magnetic Device Equipment\nDymek has long experience supporting data-storage manufacturing and magnetic thin-film applications. The market is moving from conventional hard-disk technology toward MRAM, advanced magnetic sensors and other spintronic devices, yet many core needs stay highly specialized: thin-film characterization, magnetic measurement and precision processing.\nWe provide measurement and process technologies for disk media, heads and sliders, magnetic multilayers, MRAM and GMR/TMR sensor devices.\n## Magnetic Thin-Film & Wafer Metrology\nWe provide non-contact magnetic measurement systems for magnetic multilayer wafers and thin films. Full-wafer mapping measures the magnetic properties used in development and production of data-storage, MRAM and magnetic-sensor devices.\nThin-film hysteresis and magnetoresistance measurement supports research and quality control of soft and hard magnetic materials on wafers and other samples.\nElectrical probing under an applied magnetic field adds device-level characterization of magnetic sensors, MRAM structures and other magnetically responsive devices. It links film-level magnetic properties with electrical performance.\n## Disk Media Thin-Film Metrology\nHard-disk media need precise control of ultra-thin protective and magnetic layers. We provide optical metrology that measures diamond-like carbon (DLC) thickness, optical properties and magnetic-layer thickness on disk media.\nThese measurements support process development, uniformity control and production quality assurance for advanced media.\nMicroscale optical film measurement adds local thickness and optical-property mapping on small, patterned or non-standard regions where large-spot measurement is not enough.\n## Heads, Sliders & Precision Components\nData-storage heads and sliders need tight dimensional, surface and thin-film control. We support optical and surface metrology, thin-film characterization, microscopy and other measurement methods used during head and slider processing.\nWhere special handling or automation is needed, measurement equipment can be integrated with custom fixtures and motion systems.\n## MRAM & Emerging Magnetic Memory\nMRAM manufacturing needs control of very thin magnetic multilayers, annealing and magnetic orientation across the wafer. We support magnetic wafer mapping, electrical characterization, thin-film metrology and related measurement for MRAM development and production.\nThis extends our data-storage portfolio beyond disk media into semiconductor-based non-volatile memory.\n## GMR/TMR Sensors & Magnetic Device Processing\nGiant- and tunneling-magnetoresistance (GMR/TMR) devices are increasingly used in automotive, industrial and consumer sensors. We support selective laser annealing to set magnetic reference directions on integrated GMR/TMR sensors, together with magnetic and thin-film metrology for process control.\nThe same magnetic-device expertise now serves new markets beyond traditional storage.\n## Surface, Tribology & Failure Analysis\nHead-media interaction and precision mechanical parts may need analysis of roughness, friction, wear and coating durability. We provide surface metrology, tribology and high-resolution microscopy to investigate performance and failure mechanisms.\nFor research and production, these technologies can be combined with custom handling or automation for specialized samples.\n## From Materials Development to Production\nWe combine magnetic and thin-film metrology, precision processing, surface characterization, failure analysis and custom handling around specialized storage or spintronic devices, from materials development through production quality control.\n### Not sure which technology fits?\nSend us your material, feature size and target spec. We’ll suggest a method and, where possible, a feasibility test.\n## Explore connected areas\nYield & quality\n### Metrology, Inspection & Failure Analysis\nThin-film metrology, wafer AOI, X-ray/CT, scanning acoustic microscopy, probing, SEM/TEM and more.\nAutomotive\n### Automotive & E-Mobility\nAutomated 3D inspection, tribology, EV battery X-ray/CT, SiC/GaN power semiconductor processing, MEMS sensors, LiDAR and camera optics alignment and more.\nResearch\n### Research & Academia\nElectron microscopy, micro- and nanofabrication, optics and photonics research tools, quantum device integration, 2D materials and more.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: displays-advanced-optoelectronics.html | Display Manufacturing Equipment: MicroLED Laser Lift-Off, Nanoimprint & Panel Metrology | Dymek\n# Displays & Advanced Optoelectronics Equipment\nThis sector covers panel- and module-scale optoelectronics: LCD, OLED, microLED/miniLED, smart glass, image sensors and related display or sensing products. Dymek provides process, metrology, assembly, repair, test and automation technologies from device fabrication through panel and module production.\n## MicroLED, MiniLED & Advanced Emitters\nWe support laser lift-off, transfer and local repair or rework of microLED, miniLED and related emitters. Precision positioning and automated handling let you transfer or repair individual dies or selected regions without replacing a whole panel or substrate.\nPlasma processing, thin-film deposition and wafer-level optoelectronic testing support upstream LED and microLED device fabrication. High-precision die bonding supports assembly of emitters and other optoelectronic components into modules.\n## Large-Area Patterning & Panel Processes\nLarge-area nanoimprint lithography replicates micro- and nanoscale structures across panel-size substrates for displays, wire-grid polarizers and other optical functions. The same process family supports structured surfaces in photonic and advanced display applications where optical lithography becomes inefficient over large areas.\nLaser processing and custom automation also support selective structuring, trimming and other panel-level processes that need precise, non-contact modification.\n## Panel, Film & Microstructure Metrology\nDisplay manufacturing needs measurement of transparent films, color-filter structures, photo spacers, cell gaps, retardation, local topography and other features that directly affect optical uniformity. We provide spectroscopic, interferometric and non-contact 3D metrology for these parameters, on research samples and production-scale panels.\nMeasurements cover color-filter spectral properties, multilayer film thickness, refractive index, surface roughness, panel-glass features, microlens arrays and MEMS or micromirror structures. Small-spot and automated mapping systems help where measurement must target individual patterned regions instead of averaging over a large area.\n## Optoelectronic Assembly & Sensor Packaging\nAdvanced displays and related modules can integrate emitters, detectors, image sensors, MEMS, laser sources and optics. We support high-precision die attach, flip-chip/eutectic assembly and related packaging for AR/VR modules, infrared devices, commercial lasers, CMOS image sensors and MEMS products.\nWhere optical coupling is needed, precision motion and alignment can be integrated with bonding, dispensing, curing and inspection for repeatable production. Active lens alignment also supports AR/VR optical modules, compact imaging assemblies and other sensor products that need accurate centering of multiple elements or alignment of apertures, reticles and detector planes.\n## Smart Glass, Functional Coatings & Optical Surfaces\nWe support PVD and other thin-film coating processes for display glass and smart surfaces, including:\n- Hard anti-reflective coatings\n- Transparent conductive oxides\n- Electrochromic layers and controlled transmission or color\n- Anti-fingerprint and other functional optical coatings\nLarge-area deposition platforms handle sheet and panel substrates. Precision optical coating processes apply to waveguides, sensor optics and other high-performance optical parts used alongside advanced displays.\n## Electrical, Optical & Reliability Test\nProbe systems support flat-panel displays and optoelectronic devices with electrical, optical and temperature-dependent measurements. Applications include sheet resistance, I-V and C-V characterization, RF or high-speed measurement and combined optical/electrical testing of LEDs, VCSELs, photodiodes and related devices.\nInspection, X-ray, microscopy and other analytical methods add failure analysis when defects start in packaging, interconnects or device structures rather than the visible panel surface.\n## From Process Development to Production\nNew display architectures often combine processes that no single standard tool offers. We integrate motion, machine vision, handling, metrology, repair, process equipment and software around your application, from R&D feasibility through pilot and volume automation.\n### Not sure which technology fits?\nSend us your material, feature size and target spec. We’ll suggest a method and, where possible, a feasibility test.\n## Explore connected areas\nMicro & nano optics\n### AR/VR & Wafer-Level Optics\nNanoimprint lithography, wafer-level lens molding and stacking, AR waveguide laser singulation, etch, deposition and metrology for AR/VR and more.\nPhotonic devices\n### Photonics, Optoelectronics & Optical Interconnects\nPhotonic device fabrication, wafer-level photonic integration, co-packaged optics and optical I/O, active alignment and more.\nComponents\n### Precision Optics & Optical Manufacturing\nInterferometers, non-contact 3D surface metrology, thin-film measurement, lens centration and active alignment and more.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: electronics-assembly-test-reliability.html | Electronics Assembly Inspection, EMC & Reliability Test Equipment: SMT, PCB, X-Ray | Dymek\n# Electronics Assembly, Test & Reliability\nThis sector covers what happens after semiconductor devices leave the fab: assembly into PCBs, modules and finished systems, followed by inspection, EMC development, reliability testing and failure analysis. Dymek supports SMT and PCB manufacturing, high-density interconnects, electronic modules and other finished hardware.\n## SMT, PCB & Module Inspection\nWe provide inspection technologies for solder joints, component placement, connectors, packages and other features on PCBs and electronic modules. Automated optical inspection and machine vision find visible defects and process variation. X-ray inspection reaches hidden interconnects and internal structures.\nNon-contact 3D metrology adds dimensional control of PCB substrates and fine features:\n- Line width and spacing\n- Board or substrate warpage\n- Via size, depth and position\nThis is useful during process development and incoming quality control, before assembly.\n## PCB & High-Density Interconnect Processing\nAs assemblies move to finer pitch and higher density, smaller vias and thinner multilayer structures get harder to process consistently. We support precision laser drilling and ablation for microvias and other fine PCB or interconnect features, where feature size, geometry or material mix challenge conventional methods.\nFor emerging applications, process development can add real-time monitoring and endpoint control. This improves repeatability and lowers the risk of drilling through or damaging underlying layers.\n## X-Ray & CT for Electronics\n2D X-ray, 3D CT and automated inline X-ray reveal defects you can't see from the surface. These include solder voids, bridging, insufficient solder, hidden joints, package defects and internal assembly problems.\nThese systems support process development, production quality control, incoming inspection and lab failure analysis across SMT, power electronics, automotive electronics and other assemblies.\n## EMC Development & Pre-Compliance\nElectromagnetic compatibility problems can start at IC, PCB or system level. We provide tools for emissions and immunity investigation, near-field scanning, ESD-related analysis and IC-level EMC testing.\nThey help engineers locate interference sources, understand coupling paths and improve designs before formal compliance testing.\n## RF, Wireless & High-Frequency Electronics\nRF and wireless electronics need specialized materials, packaging and test methods at frequencies where conventional electrical approaches get difficult. We support thin-film deposition for piezoelectric MEMS, BAW/FBAR and other RF devices, plus high-precision assembly of RF, microwave and hybrid-device packages.\nOn-wafer probing supports RF and sub-THz characterization alongside DC, pulsed and temperature-dependent measurements. Developers can evaluate high-frequency performance before final assembly.\n## Package & Assembly Failure Analysis\nWhen failures start inside packages or assemblies, non-destructive acoustic inspection, X-ray and high-resolution microscopy help identify delamination, voids, cracks, die-attach issues, solder defects and other hidden problems.\nElectron microscopy adds structural and materials detail for root-cause analysis. Laser-based sample preparation removes bulk material and exposes targeted regions in packages or system-level boards before microscopy or ion-beam analysis. Typical targets are large-area package cross-sections, flip-chip assemblies and solder joints or balls that are hard to prepare mechanically without deformation or artefacts.\n## Production Data & Reporting\nInspection and test equipment generates large volumes of quality data across boards, modules and finished systems. We support automated collection, correlation and reporting of inspection and test results to improve traceability and reduce manual engineering work.\nCustom scripts and software integrations process instrument data, generate reports and connect results to production databases, quality systems or other manufacturing software.\n## Materials, Coatings & Reliability\nElectronic products may also need characterization of coatings, adhesives, contact surfaces and mechanical interfaces. We support surface roughness, friction, wear, hardness, adhesion and other materials measurements during product development and reliability evaluation.\nElectrical-connector testing can link fretting wear and corrosion to real-time current, voltage and contact behavior under controlled environments. For specialized production, inspection can also be integrated with handling, machine vision and factory software.\n### Not sure which technology fits?\nSend us your material, feature size and target spec. We’ll suggest a method and, where possible, a feasibility test.\n## Related insights\nFailure Analysis\n### How Laser Sample Preparation Speeds Up Semiconductor Failure Analysis\nFailure analysis often waits on sample preparation. Laser-based preparation removes bulk material fast, so FIB time goes to the final, precise step.\n## Explore connected areas\nYield & quality\n### Metrology, Inspection & Failure Analysis\nThin-film metrology, wafer AOI, X-ray/CT, scanning acoustic microscopy, probing, SEM/TEM and more.\nAutomotive\n### Automotive & E-Mobility\nAutomated 3D inspection, tribology, EV battery X-ray/CT, SiC/GaN power semiconductor processing, MEMS sensors, LiDAR and camera optics alignment and more.\nBack-end\n### Advanced Packaging & Assembly\nHybrid bonding, wafer-to-wafer and die-to-wafer bonding, temporary bonding/debonding, die placement, thermocompression and more.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: equipment-service-fab-support.html | Semiconductor Equipment Service & Fab Support: Vacuum Pumps, RF, Furnaces | Dymek\n# Semiconductor Equipment Service & Fab Support\nFabs depend on complex equipment and subsystems that must keep running long after installation. Dymek supports selected process tools and fab subsystems through repair, refurbishment, replacement, upgrades and field service.\nOur goal is simple: help you extend equipment life, reduce unplanned downtime and keep access to critical support for current and legacy systems.\n### Not sure which technology fits?\nSend us your material, feature size and target spec. We’ll suggest a method and, where possible, a feasibility test.\n### 01 Vacuum Pump Repair, Refurbishment & Replacement\n## Vacuum Pump Repair, Refurbishment & Replacement\nVacuum pumps are critical to many deposition, etch, thermal and analytical processes. We support selected vacuum pump models through repair, refurbishment, exchange or replacement programs, depending on your installed equipment and needs.\nSupport can include evaluating failed units, coordinating overhaul work, sourcing replacements and handling logistics to your regional sites.\n### 02 RF Generator & Matching Network Support\n## RF Generator & Matching Network Support\nRF generators and matching networks are essential subsystems in plasma-based equipment. We support repair, refurbishment and replacement of selected RF power and matching equipment used in etch, deposition and related processes.\nThis gives you another path for aging or hard-to-source subsystems, so you don't have to replace an otherwise serviceable process tool.\n### 03 Legacy Furnace & Thermal Equipment Support\n## Legacy Furnace & Thermal Equipment Support\nInstalled diffusion, oxidation and LPCVD furnaces can stay useful for many years when controls, gas systems and key mechanical parts are maintained. We support selected legacy thermal equipment with upgrades, refurbishment, spare parts and technical service.\nWhere it makes sense, older systems can be modernized to improve controls, reliability or compatibility with current process requirements.\n### 04 Equipment Relocation, Installation & Field Service\n## Equipment Relocation, Installation & Field Service\nWe coordinate de-installation, relocation, re-installation, commissioning and field service for selected equipment. Our regional team supports customers running several manufacturing sites across Asia. Where needed, we coordinate with equipment manufacturers and specialist service partners.\n### 05 Spare Parts & Obsolescence Support\n## Spare Parts & Obsolescence Support\nObsolete components can cause outsized downtime on mature equipment. We help source replacement parts, identify compatible alternatives and coordinate repair or refurbishment of selected subsystems.\nFor equipment nearing end of support, we help you decide which path is most practical: refurbishment, subsystem replacement or full tool replacement.\n### 06 Flexible Support for Mature & Specialty Fabs\n## Flexible Support for Mature & Specialty Fabs\nNot every fab runs only the newest production equipment. Research fabs, MEMS lines, power semiconductor facilities and specialty manufacturers often rely on mature tools that are still technically capable but need flexible support. We work with these customers to keep useful equipment running and to find practical upgrade or replacement options.\n## Explore connected areas\nFront-end\n### Wafer Processing & Device Fabrication\nLithography, wafer bonding, plasma etch and deposition, furnaces, dicing, laser annealing, CMP and thin-film metrology for silicon, MEMS and more.\nService\n### Calibration & Service\nEquipment only creates value while it runs well. Our regional service teams keep your tools accurate, available and supported for their full working life.\nResearch\n### Research & Academia\nElectron microscopy, micro- and nanofabrication, optics and photonics research tools, quantum device integration, 2D materials and more.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: hybrid-bonding-explained.html | What Is Hybrid Bonding? W2W vs D2W Hybrid Bonding for HBM & Chiplets | Dymek\nAdvanced Packaging\n# What Is Hybrid Bonding? Wafer-to-Wafer vs Die-to-Wafer Explained\nHybrid bonding joins two chips through copper pads and dielectric simultaneously, without solder bumps. This article explains how it works, where it is used and what is required to achieve good yield.\nHybrid bonding is among the most important technologies in advanced packaging today. It enables chipmakers to stack dies at a far finer interconnect pitch than solder bumps permit. For this reason, it now underpins stacked image sensors, 3D cache chips and the roadmap for future high-bandwidth memory (HBM).\nThis guide explains what hybrid bonding is, how the wafer-to-wafer and die-to-wafer approaches differ, and the process control it requires.\n## What is hybrid bonding?\nIn a hybrid bond, two surfaces are joined in a single step through two materials simultaneously:\n- Dielectric to dielectric , typically silicon oxide or a comparable film, which bonds first at room temperature\n- Copper to copper , through pads embedded in the dielectric, which form a metallic connection during a subsequent anneal\nAs there is no solder bump, the pads can be considerably smaller and more closely spaced. Conventional micro-bumps are typically placed at pitches of tens of microns, whereas hybrid bonding operates at single-digit micron pitch and below. This allows many more connections per square millimetre, shorter signal paths and lower power per bit.\n## Wafer-to-wafer (W2W) vs die-to-wafer (D2W)\nWafer-to-wafer bonding joins two complete wafers. It offers high alignment accuracy and throughput, and is well established in stacked CMOS image sensors and certain memory designs. The trade-off is yield, since every die on one wafer is bonded to the die above it, whether good or bad.\nDie-to-wafer bonding places individual known-good dies onto a target wafer. It is well suited to chiplets and heterogeneous integration, where dies originate from different processes or differ in size. It avoids stacking defective dies, but introduces additional challenges in die handling, cleanliness and placement.\nSame-size dies, high volume\nChiplets, mixed dies, known-good die\nVery high, across the whole wafer at once\nPer die, placement-dependent\nCompounds across both wafers\nLower, as only good dies are used\nFront-end yield matching\nParticles, die handling, throughput\n## The hybrid bonding process, step by step\n- CMP. Both surfaces must be extremely flat. Copper pads are polished so that they sit very slightly recessed, allowing them to expand into contact during the anneal.\n- Cleaning. Any particle can create an unbonded void considerably larger than the particle itself.\n- Plasma activation. Activation of the dielectric surface enables strong bonding at low temperature.\n- Alignment and bonding. The two surfaces are aligned and brought into contact, with the dielectric bonding first.\n- Anneal. Heat drives copper expansion and diffusion, forming the metal joint.\n- Post-bond metrology. Inspection is carried out to check for voids, overlay error and bond strength.\n## Where hybrid bonding is used\n- Stacked image sensors , where pixel and logic wafers are bonded face to face\n- Logic-on-logic and cache stacking for high-performance processors\n- Chiplets that require dense die-to-die links\n- HBM , where it features on the roadmap for taller stacks. The research firm TrendForce reported that the major memory makers confirmed plans to use hybrid bonding for HBM5 20-high stacks, whilst HBM3e and HBM4 12-high stacks continue to use advanced MR-MUF and TC-NCF bonding.\n## What it takes to achieve yield\nHybrid bonding brings packaging closer to the discipline of the front-end fab. The principal yield factors are as follows:\n- Particle control. TrendForce also notes that particle control can increase unit costs, making cleanliness a matter of cost as well as quality.\n- Surface topography. Copper recess and dielectric flatness must be controlled at the nanometre level.\n- Overlay accuracy. Fine pitch leaves little margin for misalignment.\n- Bond inspection. Scanning acoustic microscopy and infrared inspection reveal voids, and failure analysis may require targeted cross-sections of the bonded interface.\n## How Dymek supports hybrid bonding\nDymek supports wafer-to-wafer and die-to-wafer bonding, surface preparation, activation and alignment, temporary bonding for thin wafers, and the associated metrology and failure-analysis steps. We also work with EV Group, a leading supplier of wafer bonding equipment.\nIf you are planning a hybrid bonding line or pilot, please speak with our team about your process.\n## Sources\n- TrendForce via I-Connect007: HBM5 20hi stack to adopt hybrid bonding\n## More insights\nPhotonics\n### Co-Packaged Optics (CPO) Explained: Why AI Data Centers Are Moving Optics onto the Package\nNvidia and Broadcom are now shipping co-packaged optics switches. Here is what CPO is, why it saves power, and what makes it hard to manufacture.\nFailure Analysis\n### How Laser Sample Preparation Speeds Up Semiconductor Failure Analysis\nFailure analysis often waits on sample preparation. Laser-based preparation removes bulk material fast, so FIB time goes to the final, precise step.\nMetrology\n### Thin Film Thickness Measurement: Reflectometry vs Ellipsometry vs Interferometry\nThree optical methods dominate thin-film measurement. Here is what each one measures, where it shines and how to choose.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: industrial-precision-manufacturing.html | Industrial 3D Inspection, Machine Vision & Laser Micromachining Equipment | Dymek\n# Industrial & Precision Manufacturing Solutions\nIndustrial and precision manufacturers often face inspection and process challenges that don't fit one industry. Dymek provides metrology, materials testing, laser processing, machine vision, software and automation for precision machining, foundry, marine, energy, heavy industry and other advanced manufacturing.\n## Automated 3D Inspection & Dimensional Metrology\nWe provide non-contact 3D inspection for automated dimensional measurement and surface checks on the production floor. Systems combine precision optics, machine vision, robotics and computational analysis to measure complex parts without targets or manual probing.\nApplications include castings, machined components, marine propellers, composite structures, large fabricated parts and other components that are hard to inspect automatically with conventional CMM methods.\nPortable and robot-mounted surface gauges complement full-field inspection. They measure roughness, scratches, pits, edge breaks, radii, corrosion and other local features directly on complex parts. This helps when sending a large part to a metrology lab is impractical.\n## Machine Vision & Automated Defect Inspection\nAutomated optical inspection finds scratches, contamination, surface defects, missing features and other visual problems, with less manual inspection. Machine-vision systems are configured around your part geometry, production rate and defect criteria.\nInspection can be integrated with robotics, conveyors, part handling and production software for inline or near-line quality control.\n## Materials, Coatings & Tribology\nWe provide lab systems for testing friction, wear, hardness, adhesion, roughness, coatings, lubricants and other material or surface properties. Controlled temperature, humidity, vacuum and load reproduce demanding operating environments.\nThese tools support R&D, supplier qualification, product development and quality control for bearings, gears, coatings, polymers, metals and other industrial materials.\n## Precision Laser Processing\nLaser micromachining cuts, structures, ablates or modifies metals, polymers, glass, ceramics and other materials with minimal mechanical force. Precision micro-drilling suits nozzles, filters, sieves, fluid-handling parts and other components that need very small holes, high aspect ratios or controlled hole geometry.\nWe support standard laser-processing equipment and custom process development for unusual geometries or materials, including production processes with automated handling.\n## Large & Complex Components\nLarge industrial parts bring unique metrology and handling challenges. We support inspection for wind-energy components, marine propellers, heavy castings and other large structures where portability, robot reach or full-field measurement matters.\nCustom fixtures and motion systems can be built when a part can't be positioned with standard lab equipment.\n## Data Analytics, Optimization & Custom Software\nManufacturing improvement depends more and more on how well production data is used. We support optimization, scheduling, equipment-utilization analysis, process monitoring, predictive models, dashboards and automated reporting alongside equipment and automation projects.\nCustom software processes instrument data, generates reports, connects results to production databases and automates repetitive engineering work.\n## From Feasibility to Production\nWe combine robots, precision motion, machine vision, metrology, tooling and control software into semi- or fully automated systems. This includes mechanical design, safety, part handling, recipes, data collection and factory integration.\nProjects move from feasibility testing through design, fabrication, installation and production support.\n### Not sure which technology fits?\nSend us your material, feature size and target spec. We’ll suggest a method and, where possible, a feasibility test.\n## Explore connected areas\nAerospace\n### Aerospace Manufacturing & MRO\nPortable 3D defect inspection for turbine blades, automated dimensional metrology, tribology and coating testing, failure analysis and more.\nAutomotive\n### Automotive & E-Mobility\nAutomated 3D inspection, tribology, EV battery X-ray/CT, SiC/GaN power semiconductor processing, MEMS sensors, LiDAR and camera optics alignment and more.\nService\n### Custom System Integration\nSome applications don't fit a standard tool. We build semi- or fully automated systems around your part and process, combining proven instruments with robotics, motion, machine vision, fixtures and software.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: laser-sample-preparation-failure-analysis.html | Semiconductor Failure Analysis: Laser Sample Preparation vs FIB & Mechanical | Dymek\nFailure Analysis\n# How Laser Sample Preparation Speeds Up Semiconductor Failure Analysis\nFailure analysis is frequently held up by sample preparation. Laser-based preparation removes bulk material rapidly, allowing FIB time to be reserved for the final, precise step.\nWhen a device fails, engineers must be able to see the defect. In advanced packages, that defect may be buried millimetres deep beneath silicon, mould compound, solder and copper. Reaching it is often the slowest stage of failure analysis (FA).\n## The sample preparation bottleneck\nThe usual methods of reaching a buried target are:\n- Mechanical grinding and polishing. Low in equipment cost, but slow, operator-dependent and prone to smearing, cracking or deforming soft materials such as solder.\n- Focused ion beam (FIB). Highly precise, but gallium FIB removes material slowly. Removing large volumes can take many hours.\n- Plasma FIB (PFIB). Considerably faster than gallium FIB, but still costly per cubic millimetre where targets are deep or wide.\nAs packages become larger and increasingly three-dimensional, with stacked dies, interposers and hybrid-bonded interfaces, the volume of material to be removed continues to grow.\n## Where laser preparation fits\nLaser ablation removes large volumes of material rapidly and without mechanical contact. It is used to:\n- Cut large-area package cross-sections\n- Expose flip-chip bumps, solder joints and solder balls without deformation\n- Remove MEMS caps or open up deep structures\n- Prepare targeted regions in TSVs, RDLs, interposers and hybrid-bonded stacks\n- Produce site-specific specimens for TEM, X-ray microscopy, atom-probe tomography and micromechanical testing\nThe principle is straightforward. Use the laser for bulk removal, and reserve FIB or PFIB time for the final few microns , where its precision is required.\n## A typical FA workflow\n- Fault isolation. Non-destructive methods such as X-ray, CT and scanning acoustic microscopy are used to locate the suspect area.\n- Laser bulk removal. Material is removed to a point close to the region of interest.\n- Precise end-pointing. FIB or PFIB is used to polish the final surface.\n- High-resolution analysis. SEM, TEM or other techniques are then used to reveal the defect and its cause.\n## Key considerations for a laser preparation system\n- Accurate positioning relative to the region of interest\n- Control of heat-affected zones to protect the target\n- Support for a range of sample formats, from wafers to boards\n- Straightforward transfer into your FIB, PFIB or SEM workflow\n## See it in practice\n3D-Micromac's microPREP systems are designed for this workflow. Further detail is available in the microPREP PRO video and the cross-section example .\nDymek provides support for laser sample preparation alongside X-ray, acoustic microscopy, AOI and electron microscopy. Explore our failure analysis solutions or speak with one of our engineers .\n## Sources\n- 3D-Micromac: Dymek trade agency announcement\n- 3D-Micromac microPREP PRO video\n## More insights\nAdvanced Packaging\n### What Is Hybrid Bonding? Wafer-to-Wafer vs Die-to-Wafer Explained\nHybrid bonding joins two chips through copper pads and dielectric at the same time, with no solder bumps. Here is how it works, where it is used and what it takes to get yield.\nPhotonics\n### Co-Packaged Optics (CPO) Explained: Why AI Data Centers Are Moving Optics onto the Package\nNvidia and Broadcom are now shipping co-packaged optics switches. Here is what CPO is, why it saves power, and what makes it hard to manufacture.\nMetrology\n### Thin Film Thickness Measurement: Reflectometry vs Ellipsometry vs Interferometry\nThree optical methods dominate thin-film measurement. Here is what each one measures, where it shines and how to choose.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: life-sciences-diagnostics-medical-devices.html | Life Sciences, Diagnostics & Medical Device Manufacturing Equipment | Dymek\n# Life Sciences, Diagnostics & Medical Device Equipment\nLife-science research and product manufacturing increasingly combine advanced microscopy, precision manufacturing, microfluidics, optical systems and automated quality control. Dymek supports researchers and manufacturers of diagnostic, genomic and medical-device products across imaging, microfabrication, inspection and materials characterization.\n## Advanced Microscopy & Biological Imaging\nWe provide scanning and transmission electron microscopy solutions for high-resolution imaging of biological samples. Applications range from surface morphology and cellular ultrastructure to tissue imaging, volume electron microscopy, tomography and structural biology.\nElectron microscopy complements optical and fluorescence methods by revealing structures at nanometer resolution. It supports research in cell biology, microbiology, neuroscience, pathology, biomaterials and related fields.\n## Biomedical Optical Metrology & Assembly\nOptical metrology supports biomedical and ophthalmic applications that need fast, non-contact measurement. Examples include contact lenses and lens molds, and research measurements of the corneal surface and tear-film thickness, where subject motion can limit conventional interferometry.\nPrecision active alignment also supports medical optical assemblies such as endoscopes and microscope objectives. In these, the centering and tilt of each lens element directly affect image quality and repeatability.\n## Genomics & Diagnostic Manufacturing\nDiagnostic and sequencing consumables often contain dense arrays of small features that need consistent manufacturing quality. We provide automated optical inspection and defect-analysis technologies for precision substrates, patterned surfaces and other diagnostic components.\nAI-assisted inspection helps manufacturers find particles, surface defects, process variation and other anomalies with less manual review.\n### Not sure which technology fits?\nSend us your material, feature size and target spec. We’ll suggest a method and, where possible, a feasibility test.\n## Microfluidics, Biochips & Lab-on-Chip\nMicrofluidic and lab-on-chip devices put channels, chambers, sensors and functional surfaces into compact substrates. We support wafer bonding, nanoimprint, hot embossing, lithography, surface processing and other fabrication methods for microfluidic devices.\nApplications include:\n- Blood and DNA analysis\n- Biosensors and cell sorting\n- Organ-on-chip platforms\n- Drug-delivery research\nNon-contact surface and volumetric metrology measures microfluidic cells, transparent layers and internal interfaces without touching delicate structures. Surface topography, layer thickness and subsurface geometry can be checked during process development or quality control.\n## Precision Glass & Internal Channel Structures\nGlass is widely used in diagnostics, microfluidics and optical-biological devices thanks to its chemical stability, optical properties and compatibility with microfabrication. We support precision laser cutting, micro-drilling and structuring of glass for diagnostic slides, biochips and specialized devices. This includes through-holes and fluidic access features, where conventional machining can cause chipping, cracking or excess stress.\nFor emerging applications, internal channel structures inside glass can also be explored for fluid handling, thermal control and other functions that are hard to machine conventionally.\n## Biomedical Thin Films & Nanomaterials\nThin-film metrology measures local coating thickness and optical properties on small or patterned biomedical parts. Applications include parylene coatings, polymer films and balloon walls or other thin transparent structures that need microscale measurement spots.\nFor research, vacuum-deposited nanoparticle layers support studies in drug delivery, antimicrobial and antiviral surfaces, biofilms, biosensors and other nanomedicine or diagnostic concepts. We support flexible R&D deposition systems for researchers developing these materials.\n## Biomaterials & Medical-Device Tribology\nWe support characterization of medical-device materials, coatings and interfaces through surface metrology, friction and wear testing, scratch and adhesion measurement and other mechanical tests.\nApplications include implants, catheters, coatings, polymers and other parts where surface behavior and durability affect performance. For catheters and other minimally invasive devices, testing quantifies lubricity and insertion, push, navigation and removal forces, and links them to changes in surface condition. Custom fixtures, inspection and automation support scale-up where standard equipment does not fit.\n## Explore connected areas\nResearch\n### Research & Academia\nElectron microscopy, micro- and nanofabrication, optics and photonics research tools, quantum device integration, 2D materials and more.\nComponents\n### Precision Optics & Optical Manufacturing\nInterferometers, non-contact 3D surface metrology, thin-film measurement, lens centration and active alignment and more.\nIndustrial\n### Industrial & Precision Manufacturing\nAutomated 3D inspection beyond CMM, portable surface gauges, machine vision AOI, tribology and coating testing, laser micro-drilling and more.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: metrology-equipment-sales.html | Metrology & Inspection Equipment Sales in Asia | Dymek\n# Metrology & Inspection Equipment Sales\nWe connect you with measurement, inspection and process equipment from our principals in Europe, the USA, Japan and South Korea. Our engineers help you choose the right tool for your application, then support it on the ground.\n## What we supply\nOur portfolio covers the measurement and inspection needs of advanced manufacturing:\n- Interferometric and dynamic optical metrology\n- Thin-film thickness and optical-property measurement\n- Non-contact 3D surface and topography metrology\n- X-ray/CT, scanning acoustic microscopy and automated optical inspection\n- Electron microscopy and laser sample preparation\n- Electrical, optoelectronic and magnetic probing\n- Tribology and materials characterization\nProcess equipment for bonding, lithography, nanoimprint, deposition, thermal and laser processing is also available. Browse by industry to see what fits your application.\n## How we help you buy with confidence\n- Application review. We start with your part, process and specification, not a catalogue.\n- Feasibility trials. Where possible, we arrange sample measurements or demos so you can see results on your own parts.\n- Clear proposals. We explain options and trade-offs in plain language, so your engineering and purchasing teams can decide together.\n- Import and logistics. Our regional offices handle the local steps of bringing equipment to your site.\n- Installation and training. We commission the system and train your operators and engineers.\n- Long-term support. Service, spare parts and upgrades stay close to you through our regional network.\n## Why buy through Dymek\nSince 1989, we have worked as the link between global technology providers and industrial customers across Asia. You get local language support, local time zones and engineers who understand both the technology and your production reality.\n## Where this service applies\nYield & quality\n### Metrology, Inspection & Failure Analysis\nThin-film metrology, wafer AOI, X-ray/CT, scanning acoustic microscopy, probing, SEM/TEM and more.\nComponents\n### Precision Optics & Optical Manufacturing\nInterferometers, non-contact 3D surface metrology, thin-film measurement, lens centration and active alignment and more.\nService\n### Calibration & Service\nEquipment only creates value while it runs well. Our regional service teams keep your tools accurate, available and supported for their full working life.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: metrology-inspection-failure-analysis.html | Semiconductor Metrology, Inspection & Failure Analysis Equipment | Dymek\n# Metrology, Inspection & Failure Analysis\nBetter yield depends on two things: catching variation early, and finding root causes fast when failures happen. Dymek provides metrology, inspection, manufacturing-data and failure-analysis solutions for wafers, thin films, packages and assembled electronic systems.\nWe support you from process development through production and reliability analysis.\n## Thin-Film & Optical Process Metrology\nNon-contact optical metrology measures film thickness, refractive index, extinction coefficient, reflectance, transmittance and multilayer structures. It works across dielectrics, resists, semiconductor layers and transparent films for mapping, uniformity control and production monitoring.\nSmall-spot reflectometry and non-contact 3D/OCT metrology extend this to patterned regions, rough surfaces, transparent films and subsurface interfaces. Examples include photoresist, MEMS, LED, VCSEL and advanced-packaging structures.\nInterferometric surface metrology adds wafer flatness, thickness variation and roughness measurement close to the process, including areas where vibration isolation is limited.\n## Wafer-Level Automated Optical Inspection\nAutomated optical inspection (AOI) finds particles, scratches, pattern defects, process abnormalities and other visual anomalies. It works on substrates, bare wafers, patterned wafers and packaged devices.\nAI-assisted defect detection and classification can reduce manual review, improve consistency and link inspection results to process optimization. Systems can be configured for novel materials, compound semiconductors, photonics and MEMS as well as conventional silicon.\n## X-Ray & CT Inspection\nX-ray inspection gives non-destructive access to buried structures inside semiconductor packages and electronic assemblies. We support 2D X-ray, 3D CT and automated inline inspection for solder joints, micro-bumps, Cu pillars, TSV-related structures, die attach, package voids and other internal features.\nHigh-resolution systems suit laboratory analysis, process development or production inspection.\n## Scanning Acoustic Microscopy\nAcoustic microscopy is especially good at finding planar defects and interface problems inside bonded wafers and packaged devices. Typical findings include:\n- Delamination and voids\n- Non-bonded interfaces and die cracks\n- Underfill quality and molding defects\n- Thermal-interface layer issues\nBecause the method is non-destructive, you can use it for process characterization, reliability analysis and production screening.\n## Electrical, Magnetic & Device Characterization\nThis includes four-point-probe resistivity, wafer and chip probing, optoelectronic testing and magnetic thin-film measurement for MRAM and sensors. Probe systems range from ultra-low-signal I-V/C-V to high-current, high-voltage power devices, RF/sub-THz, magnetic-field-dependent and temperature-controlled testing.\n## Electron Microscopy & High-Resolution Analysis\nSome defects need high-resolution structural or materials analysis. Scanning and transmission electron microscopy (SEM and TEM) provide detailed imaging and compositional information. They support process development, contamination analysis, package investigations, materials characterization and root-cause analysis.\n## Laser Sample Preparation for Failure Analysis\nTargeted sample preparation is often the slowest step in failure analysis. We provide laser-based preparation systems that remove large volumes of material quickly and expose regions of interest in wafers, advanced packages and system-level assemblies.\nApplications include:\n- Large-area package cross-sections\n- Flip-chip and solder structures\n- MEMS cap removal and deep-structure access\n- Targeted preparation of TSVs, RDLs, interposers, hybrid-bonded stacks and other buried interfaces\nFast bulk removal can cut FIB or PFIB time before precise end-pointing, final polishing and downstream SEM, TEM, X-ray or atom-probe analysis.\n## Manufacturing Data, Yield Analytics & Software\nManufacturing software brings together fabrication, metrology, inspection, assembly and test data. Use it for yield analytics, defect management, SPC, automated reporting, outlier detection, lot disposition, wafer and assembly mapping and equipment monitoring.\n## From Measurement Development to Production\nWe can take a measurement or inspection requirement from feasibility testing to a repeatable production workflow. We integrate instruments with handling, robotics, machine vision, fixtures and factory software. Results can feed databases, pass/fail logic and custom reporting or analysis scripts.\n### Not sure which technology fits?\nSend us your material, feature size and target spec. We’ll suggest a method and, where possible, a feasibility test.\n## Related insights\nFailure Analysis\n### How Laser Sample Preparation Speeds Up Semiconductor Failure Analysis\nFailure analysis often waits on sample preparation. Laser-based preparation removes bulk material fast, so FIB time goes to the final, precise step.\nMetrology\n### Thin Film Thickness Measurement: Reflectometry vs Ellipsometry vs Interferometry\nThree optical methods dominate thin-film measurement. Here is what each one measures, where it shines and how to choose.\n## Explore connected areas\nFront-end\n### Wafer Processing & Device Fabrication\nLithography, wafer bonding, plasma etch and deposition, furnaces, dicing, laser annealing, CMP and thin-film metrology for silicon, MEMS and more.\nBack-end\n### Advanced Packaging & Assembly\nHybrid bonding, wafer-to-wafer and die-to-wafer bonding, temporary bonding/debonding, die placement, thermocompression and more.\nElectronics\n### Electronics Assembly, Test & Reliability\nSMT and PCB inspection, X-ray and CT for electronics, laser microvia drilling, EMC pre-compliance, RF and sub-THz probing and more.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: optics-photonics.html | Optics & Photonics Manufacturing, Metrology & Test Equipment in Asia | Dymek\n# Optics & Photonics Equipment and Engineering\nEquipment and engineering for optical manufacturing, metrology, alignment and test. Our portfolio covers lenses, mirrors and coatings through to AR/VR waveguides, integrated photonics, optical interconnects and co-packaged optics.\n## Where are you in the process?\n### Precision Optics & Optical Manufacturing\nConventional optical components and assemblies such as lenses, mirrors, windows, filters and coated optics. Capabilities include interferometric and surface metrology, thin-film characterization, lens alignment, coating deposition and precision glass processing, from fabrication through final optical assembly.\n### AR/VR & Wafer-Level Optics\nWafer-scale manufacture of micro- and nanostructured optics, including AR/VR waveguides, diffractive elements, microlens arrays and compact wafer-level optical systems. Technologies include nanoimprint, lens replication and stacking, high-index glass processing, deposition, plasma processing and process metrology.\n### Photonics, Optoelectronics & Optical Interconnects\nActive and passive photonic devices and optical communication hardware, including lasers, VCSELs, photodetectors, PICs, optical sensors, optical I/O and CPO. Capabilities run from device fabrication and wafer-level integration through optical alignment, packaging and electrical/optical test.\nAcross all paths\n### Custom & Emerging Optical Applications\nSome optical designs don't have a standard process flow yet. For these, we support feasibility testing, custom fixtures and tooling, precision motion and alignment, automated handling, machine vision, metrology integration and production automation.\n## Common questions\n### What optics and photonics applications does Dymek support?\nPrecision optics manufacturing, AR/VR and wafer-level optics, and photonic devices and optical interconnects, plus custom optical processes.\n### Can Dymek help with an optical process that has no standard equipment?\nYes. We support feasibility testing, custom fixtures and tooling, precision motion and alignment, machine vision and production automation.\n## Explore connected areas\nDisplays\n### Displays & Advanced Optoelectronics\nMicroLED and miniLED laser lift-off, transfer and repair, large-area nanoimprint, panel and color-filter metrology and more.\nAerospace\n### Aerospace Manufacturing & MRO\nPortable 3D defect inspection for turbine blades, automated dimensional metrology, tribology and coating testing, failure analysis and more.\nResearch\n### Research & Academia\nElectron microscopy, micro- and nanofabrication, optics and photonics research tools, quantum device integration, 2D materials and more.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: photonics-optoelectronics-optical-interconnects.html | Photonics & Co-Packaged Optics Manufacturing Equipment: PIC, VCSEL, Optical I/O | Dymek\n# Photonics, Optoelectronics & Optical Interconnects\nThis sector covers photonic devices, integrated optical systems and high-speed optical communication hardware. Dymek provides process, integration, alignment and test technologies for lasers, VCSELs, LEDs, photodetectors, optical sensors, waveguides, PICs, optical I/O and co-packaged optics (CPO).\n### Not sure which technology fits?\nSend us your material, feature size and target spec. We’ll suggest a method and, where possible, a feasibility test.\n## Photonic Device Fabrication\nPhotonic devices are made from many materials, including silicon-based materials, compound semiconductors and optical crystals. We provide plasma etching, deposition, wafer cleaning, surface treatment and precision laser processing for photonic device fabrication.\nProcesses support lasers, waveguides, detectors, microLEDs, gratings and other optical or optoelectronic structures. Laser lift-off also supports microLED and other compound semiconductor devices where functional layers must be separated from growth or carrier substrates.\nPVD thin-film processes support photonic integrated circuits, optical interference stacks and specialized device materials. Flexible research platforms can also deposit superconducting or functional films for emerging quantum-photonic and sensor structures.\n## Wafer-Level Integration & Photonic Packaging\nModern photonic systems combine optical, electronic and mechanical structures in tightly integrated packages. We support wafer bonding, thin-wafer processing, lithography, nanoimprint and precision assembly for photonic integration.\nApplications include optical coupling structures, grating couplers, beam-shaping elements, heterogeneous photonic integration and other architectures that combine several materials and device functions.\nPrecision assembly supports commercial laser, infrared, LiDAR, MEMS/CIS and optical-communication modules, using high-accuracy die placement and suitable bonding methods. For multi-element optics, active alignment centers lenses, collimators, apertures, reticles and detector planes across visible and infrared wavelengths.\n## Co-Packaged Optics & Optical I/O\nAI infrastructure and high-performance computing are pulling optical interconnects closer to electronic processors. We support manufacturing processes for co-packaged optics and optical I/O, including wafer and die integration, optical coupling structures, precision alignment and photonic packaging.\nAlignment technologies support lenses, fiber arrays and other optical interfaces that need precise positioning between photonic devices and external optical connections.\n## Photonic Process Metrology\nPhotonic manufacturing often needs dimensional and layer measurements in addition to optical testing. We support non-contact 3D topography, transparent-film thickness and multilayer or subsurface metrology for photonic wafers and device structures.\nApplications include patterned photoresist on silicon or compound semiconductor wafers, post-development and pre-etch checks, transparent functional layers and microfabricated features in VCSELs, LEDs, MEMS and other photonic devices.\n## Optoelectronic Device Test\nOptoelectronic devices need both electrical and optical characterization. We provide wafer- and chip-level probe systems for combined measurement of VCSELs, laser diodes, LEDs, photodiodes and avalanche photodiodes.\nDepending on the device, measurements include:\n- Electrical characteristics\n- Emitted optical power and wavelength\n- Near- and far-field beam behavior\n- Frequency response\n## Manufacturing Data & Yield Analytics\nManufacturing software brings photonics data together across wafer fabrication, optical/electrical wafer test, assembly, burn-in and final test. Yield analytics, automated reporting and custom integrations link wafer-level measurements to assembly conditions and final device performance.\n## Precision Optical Alignment\nPhotonic modules often need micron or sub-micron alignment of lenses, fibers and optical axes. Passive or active alignment, precision motion, component handling and custom fixtures can be integrated with bonding, dispensing, curing and inspection.\n## Quantum Photonics & Emerging Integration\nEmerging photonic and quantum devices can require low-temperature heterogeneous integration of materials such as silicon, lithium niobate and III-V semiconductors. These are hard to combine through conventional growth alone.\n## Fiber Optics & Specialized Photonic Manufacturing\nSpecialized photonic manufacturing can also include optical-fiber preform fabrication and related optical-communication processes.\n## From Photonic R&D to Production\nFor emerging photonic applications where standard equipment is not the full answer, we combine process equipment, alignment, metrology, fixtures, handling and automation around the workflow you need.\n## Related insights\nPhotonics\n### Co-Packaged Optics (CPO) Explained: Why AI Data Centers Are Moving Optics onto the Package\nNvidia and Broadcom are now shipping co-packaged optics switches. Here is what CPO is, why it saves power, and what makes it hard to manufacture.\n## Explore connected areas\nComponents\n### Precision Optics & Optical Manufacturing\nInterferometers, non-contact 3D surface metrology, thin-film measurement, lens centration and active alignment and more.\nAI & HPC\n### AI Computing & Data Center Hardware\nHybrid bonding for HBM and chiplets, co-packaged optics and optical I/O, advanced package inspection, failure analysis and more.\nBack-end\n### Advanced Packaging & Assembly\nHybrid bonding, wafer-to-wafer and die-to-wafer bonding, temporary bonding/debonding, die placement, thermocompression and more.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: photovoltaics-solar-manufacturing.html | Solar Cell Manufacturing Equipment: Laser Processing, Diffusion & PV Metrology | Dymek\n# Photovoltaics & Solar Cell Manufacturing Equipment\nPhotovoltaic manufacturing combines wafer processing, thin-film deposition, laser processing, precision inspection and high-throughput automation. Dymek provides equipment and engineering for crystalline-silicon and thin-film solar-cell research and production.\nOur technologies support single process steps as well as pilot-line development, materials research and specialized next-generation photovoltaic structures.\n### Not sure which technology fits?\nSend us your material, feature size and target spec. We’ll suggest a method and, where possible, a feasibility test.\n## Thermal Processing, Diffusion & Deposition\nWe provide furnace and thermal-process equipment for diffusion, oxidation, LPCVD and other high-temperature steps in photovoltaic and semiconductor processing. Systems support R&D, pilot production and selected high-volume applications.\nPlasma and vacuum deposition technologies support passivation, functional films and other materials needed in solar-cell structures.\nPVD platforms also support solar-absorber and functional thin-film development, with flexible sputtering or evaporation setups for research and scalable production.\n## Laser Processing of Solar Cells\nLaser processing gives high precision without mechanical contact and is widely used in modern PV manufacturing. We support laser contact opening, cell separation, scribing, structuring and other micromachining for crystalline and thin-film solar technologies.\nThermal laser separation divides full silicon cells into half-cells or other formats while minimizing edge damage and keeping mechanical strength.\n## Flexible & Thin-Film Photovoltaics\nFlexible PV materials may need high-speed roll-to-roll patterning of several functional layers. We support laser scribing and isolation for thin-film technologies on flexible substrates, including custom roll-to-roll setups.\nApplications span CIGS, perovskite and other emerging thin-film technologies that need precise, non-contact patterning.\n## Thin-Film & Optical Metrology\nSolar-cell performance depends on the thickness and optical properties of many deposited layers. We provide reflectometry, spectrophotometry, ellipsometry and other optical thin-film measurement for film thickness, refractive index, extinction coefficient, reflectance and transmittance.\nThese measurements support process development, uniformity mapping and production control for coatings, passivation layers, transparent conductive films and other solar-cell materials.\nElectrical characterization complements optical metrology. Four-point-probe measurement gives sheet resistance and related properties on wafers, conductive films and cell structures. Wafer- and cell-level probe systems support electrical and optoelectronic characterization of PV devices, including I-V measurement during research, process development and reliability testing.\n## Advanced & Multi-Junction Solar Research\nNext-generation solar cells increasingly combine dissimilar semiconductors, engineered substrates and complex layer stacks. Wafer bonding and heterogeneous integration support research into tandem and multi-junction devices where growing all materials in one structure is difficult. This includes low-temperature integration of III-V layers with silicon and other substrates.\nWe work with research institutes and manufacturers to evaluate specialized bonding, deposition, laser-processing and metrology needs for emerging PV architectures.\nNanoparticle deposition also supports exploratory work in light harvesting, photovoltaics, catalysis and related energy-conversion materials where particle composition and size are part of the research question.\n## From Process Development to Production\nFor production, laser processing, inspection and metrology can be integrated with automated handling, recipe control, data collection and factory interfaces. We support feasibility trials, custom fixtures and process development for non-standard formats or emerging materials before scale-up.\n## Explore connected areas\nFront-end\n### Wafer Processing & Device Fabrication\nLithography, wafer bonding, plasma etch and deposition, furnaces, dicing, laser annealing, CMP and thin-film metrology for silicon, MEMS and more.\nResearch\n### Research & Academia\nElectron microscopy, micro- and nanofabrication, optics and photonics research tools, quantum device integration, 2D materials and more.\nIndustrial\n### Industrial & Precision Manufacturing\nAutomated 3D inspection beyond CMM, portable surface gauges, machine vision AOI, tribology and coating testing, laser micro-drilling and more.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: precision-optics-optical-manufacturing.html | Precision Optics Manufacturing: Interferometry, Lens Alignment & Optical Coating | Dymek\n# Precision Optics & Optical Manufacturing Equipment\nPrecision optical components need tight control of geometry, surface quality, coatings and alignment. Dymek provides equipment to make, characterize and assemble lenses, mirrors, windows, prisms, filters and other optical components, from process development to final inspection and assembly.\n## Optical Metrology\nWe provide interferometric measurement systems for precision optical components and complete optical systems. They measure surface form, radius of curvature, transmitted wavefront and other parameters that verify optical performance.\nSystems are available for flat, spherical, aspheric and other challenging surfaces, at visible and infrared wavelengths. Vibration-insensitive technology allows accurate measurement directly in manufacturing areas, so parts don't need to travel to an isolated metrology lab.\nNon-contact 3D surface metrology adds production measurement for aspheric and freeform optics, diamond-turned parts and molds, microlens arrays and molded lenses. Depending on the geometry, measurements include:\n- Sag and form\n- Front-to-back vertex offset\n- Center thickness and total-thickness variation\n- Wedge and local feature depth\n- Tray-based inspection of multiple optical elements\n## Thin-Film & Optical Characterization\nOptical coatings need precise control of film thickness and optical properties. Our thin-film systems use reflectometry, spectrophotometry and ellipsometry to measure coating thickness, refractive index, extinction coefficient, reflectance and transmittance.\nThey support anti-reflective coatings, optical filters, mirrors, transparent films and multilayer optical structures.\n## Surface & Mechanical Characterization\nOptical performance can also depend on surface roughness, coating durability and mechanical properties. We provide equipment to measure roughness, topography, friction, wear, hardness, adhesion, scratch resistance and coating performance.\nThese measurements support process development and reliability testing for optical glass, coated components and other precision surfaces.\n## Lens Alignment & Optical Assembly\nAccurate alignment is critical for multi-element optical systems. Our precision systems measure and align lens centration, tilt, optical axis, radius of curvature and element position during assembly.\nEach optical surface can be measured during assembly. That lets you position lenses quantitatively before bonding or fixing, instead of relying only on mechanical references or final system tests. Applications include singlets, cemented lenses, air-spaced assemblies and complex multi-element systems.\nActive alignment also supports production inspection of finished lens stacks. It measures beam deviation, radius of curvature, vertex position and air gaps, and aligns reticles, apertures or detector planes inside an assembly.\n## Optical Coatings & Thin-Film Deposition\nWe provide vacuum deposition equipment for optical coating research, process development and production. Technologies include sputtering, electron-beam and thermal evaporation and other physical vapor deposition (PVD) processes.\nApplications include anti-reflective and high-reflective coatings, optical filters, mirrors, beam-control coatings and other functional thin films across ultraviolet, visible and infrared wavelengths.\nSpecialized coating approaches also support:\n- Low-loss and high laser-damage-threshold optics\n- Infrared imaging and thermal optics\n- Protective diamond-like carbon (DLC) coatings\n- Precision coatings on lenses and other 3D optical shapes\nThese serve laser systems, imaging, spectroscopy, sensing and demanding environmental applications.\n## Glass & Optical Component Processing\nPrecision laser micromachining is a flexible, contactless way to process glass, sapphire and other transparent or brittle materials. We support laser cutting, structuring, ablation and selective removal of thin films on optical substrates.\nLaser processes produce complex shapes with minimal mechanical load. They can be integrated with automated handling and inspection for production.\n## From Optical Process Development to Production\nFor unusual optical components or new manufacturing processes, we evaluate feasibility first. Then we combine custom fixtures, motion, handling, inspection and automation around the chosen process, from lab development to integrated production cells.\n### Not sure which technology fits?\nSend us your material, feature size and target spec. We’ll suggest a method and, where possible, a feasibility test.\n## Related insights\nMetrology\n### Thin Film Thickness Measurement: Reflectometry vs Ellipsometry vs Interferometry\nThree optical methods dominate thin-film measurement. Here is what each one measures, where it shines and how to choose.\n## Explore connected areas\nMicro & nano optics\n### AR/VR & Wafer-Level Optics\nNanoimprint lithography, wafer-level lens molding and stacking, AR waveguide laser singulation, etch, deposition and metrology for AR/VR and more.\nAerospace\n### Aerospace Manufacturing & MRO\nPortable 3D defect inspection for turbine blades, automated dimensional metrology, tribology and coating testing, failure analysis and more.\nService\n### Metrology Equipment Sales\nWe connect you with measurement, inspection and process equipment from our principals in Europe, the USA, Japan and South Korea. Our engineers help you choose the right tool for your application, then support it on the ground.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: research-academia.html | Research Equipment for Universities & Cleanrooms: SEM, Nanofabrication, Probing | Dymek\n# Research & Academia Equipment\nUniversities, national laboratories and research institutes routinely cross industry lines. Dymek provides research equipment and engineering support for shared facilities, cleanrooms, core laboratories and individual groups working across semiconductors, photonics, materials, energy, life sciences and emerging fields.\n## Electron Microscopy & Advanced Imaging\nScanning and transmission electron microscopy support high-resolution imaging, elemental and structural analysis, biological ultrastructure, tomography, failure analysis and other workflows. Samples span semiconductors, metals, ceramics, polymers, batteries and biological materials.\n## Micro- & Nanofabrication\nWe support cleanroom and lab processes including lithography, wafer bonding, nanoimprint, plasma etching, deposition, thermal processing, CMP process development and laser micromachining. Equipment is available for research and process development across semiconductors, MEMS, photonics, microfluidics and other microfabricated devices.\nFlexible R&D platforms let researchers explore new materials, substrates and process flows before moving to pilot or production scale.\n## Optics & Photonics Research\nWe provide interferometric optical metrology, thin-film characterization, precision alignment, optical coating equipment, photonic device processing and wafer-level optics technologies.\nApplications include precision optics, lasers, waveguides, AR/VR structures, integrated photonics, optical sensors and other experimental optical systems.\nGroups building optical instruments can use active alignment to assemble microscope objectives, laser collimators, telescope-related optics and other multi-element systems. Dynamic interferometry supports adaptive optics, MEMS and measurement in vibration-prone or environmental test setups.\n## Quantum & Emerging Device Research\nEmerging devices often need materials and process flows that are hard to reproduce with conventional semiconductor methods. We support low-temperature wafer bonding and heterogeneous integration for photonic and quantum-device research, combining silicon, lithium niobate, III-V materials and other specialized platforms.\nThin-film deposition platforms add superconducting films, photonic and sensor layers, piezoelectric materials and wide-bandgap metallization. This gives flexible R&D capability for quantum, RF, MEMS, power-electronics and photonic-device research.\nFor advanced-materials research, characterization can run under controlled environments, including vacuum, cryogenic or high temperature, humidity and hydrogen exposure. Use it to study friction, wear, coatings and interfaces under realistic conditions.\n## 2D Materials, Nanoparticles & Emerging Materials\nWe support thin-film deposition, low-damage plasma processing and other tools for graphene, 2D materials and emerging functional materials. Controlled low-power processing helps where normal plasma conditions would damage delicate layers or contaminate devices.\nVacuum nanoparticle deposition supports research in:\n- Nanomedicine and biosensors\n- Photovoltaics and light harvesting\n- Fuel-cell catalysis and electrochemistry\n- Environmental purification and surface-enhanced spectroscopies\nSystems offer controlled particle size, composition and co-deposition for exploratory materials research.\n## Materials & Surface Characterization\nResearch labs often need several methods to understand materials and surfaces. We support thin-film thickness and optical-property measurement, tribology, roughness and topography, hardness, adhesion, magnetic characterization, electrical measurement and other analytical techniques.\nThese tools serve materials science, coatings, energy research, electronics, biomaterials and many other disciplines.\n## Life Sciences & Microfluidics\nFor biological and diagnostic research, we support electron microscopy, microfluidic fabrication, nanoimprint, precision glass processing and automated inspection. Applications include cell and tissue imaging, biochips, lab-on-chip systems, diagnostic substrates and emerging internal-channel structures in glass.\n## Failure Analysis & Sample Preparation\nResearch and shared facilities may also need tools to investigate failed devices or prepare difficult samples. We support X-ray, acoustic inspection, automated optical inspection, electron microscopy and laser-based sample preparation for SEM, TEM, X-ray microscopy, atom-probe tomography and micromechanical testing. Laser preparation produces targeted cross-sections, site-specific specimens and custom sample geometries.\n## Advanced Electrical & Device Probing\nFlexible probe systems support device research from ultra-low-current and capacitance measurement through high-current, high-voltage power devices, RF and sub-THz characterization, optoelectronic testing and magnetic-field-dependent measurement. Temperature-controlled setups extend testing from cryogenic conditions to several hundred degrees Celsius, depending on the application.\n## Custom Research Systems & Experimental Applications\nNovel research doesn't always fit standard equipment. We help evaluate new applications and develop custom fixtures, sample holders, motion, handling, machine vision, integrated experimental systems and data-processing or reporting software.\nThis flexibility supports exploratory work and gives you a path to repeatable lab, pilot or future production workflows as your application matures.\n### Not sure which technology fits?\nSend us your material, feature size and target spec. We’ll suggest a method and, where possible, a feasibility test.\n## Go deeper into your field\nEach technical page explains processes and applications in detail.\n## Explore connected areas\nFront-end\n### Wafer Processing & Device Fabrication\nLithography, wafer bonding, plasma etch and deposition, furnaces, dicing, laser annealing, CMP and thin-film metrology for silicon, MEMS and more.\nOptics & Photonics\n### Optics & Photonics\nEquipment for precision optics, AR/VR waveguides and wafer-level optics, photonic devices and more.\nLife sciences\n### Life Sciences, Diagnostics & Medical Devices\nElectron microscopy for biology, microfluidic and lab-on-chip fabrication, diagnostic substrate inspection, precision glass processing and more.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: semiconductor-manufacturing-advanced-packaging.html | Semiconductor Manufacturing & Advanced Packaging Equipment in Asia | Dymek\n# Semiconductor Manufacturing & Advanced Packaging Equipment\nEquipment, software and engineering support from the first wafer process to the finished package. We serve R&D labs, pilot lines and volume fabs working with silicon, compound semiconductors, power devices, MEMS and sensors.\n## Where are you in the process?\n### Wafer Processing & Device Fabrication\nLithography and resist processing, wafer bonding and layer transfer, plasma etch and deposition, thermal processing, cleaning, wafer separation and precision laser processing. Process-control metrology can be added around each step for silicon, MEMS, compound semiconductors, SiC/GaN power devices, sensors and other specialty wafers.\n### Advanced Packaging & Assembly\nWafer-to-wafer and die-to-wafer bonding, hybrid bonding, temporary bonding and debonding, thin-wafer handling, precision die placement, flip-chip and thermocompression assembly, and 2.5D/3D integration. Typical applications are chiplets, HBM, fan-out, SiP, wafer-level and optical packaging.\n### Metrology, Inspection & Failure Analysis\nOptical thin-film measurement, automated wafer inspection, X-ray/CT, scanning acoustic microscopy, electrical probing, electron microscopy and surface characterization. Failure-analysis workflows can also include fast laser sample preparation before SEM, FIB, TEM or other high-resolution analysis.\nAcross all paths\n### Manufacturing Data & Yield Analytics\nManufacturing software brings together data from fabrication, metrology, inspection, assembly and test. Use it for yield analytics, defect management, SPC, wafer and assembly mapping, automated reporting and equipment monitoring. Custom scripts and integrations can handle specialized engineering or reporting workflows.\n### Equipment Service & Fab Support\nRepair, refurbishment, replacement, upgrades, field service, spare parts and relocation support for selected vacuum pumps, RF generators and matching networks, legacy thermal equipment and related subsystems.\n## Common questions\n### Which semiconductor processes does Dymek support?\nDymek supports wafer processing (lithography, bonding, etch, deposition, thermal processing, dicing and laser processing), advanced packaging, metrology and inspection, failure analysis, manufacturing data software and fab equipment service.\n### Does Dymek support R&D as well as production?\nYes. Our technologies serve R&D laboratories, pilot lines and volume manufacturing.\n### Where does Dymek provide support?\nDymek has offices in Hong Kong, mainland China, Taiwan, Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand, Vietnam and the Philippines.\n## Explore connected areas\nAI & HPC\n### AI Computing & Data Center Hardware\nHybrid bonding for HBM and chiplets, co-packaged optics and optical I/O, advanced package inspection, failure analysis and more.\nAutomotive\n### Automotive & E-Mobility\nAutomated 3D inspection, tribology, EV battery X-ray/CT, SiC/GaN power semiconductor processing, MEMS sensors, LiDAR and camera optics alignment and more.\nPhotonic devices\n### Photonics, Optoelectronics & Optical Interconnects\nPhotonic device fabrication, wafer-level photonic integration, co-packaged optics and optical I/O, active alignment and more.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: sic-gan-power-device-manufacturing.html | SiC & GaN Power Device Manufacturing Challenges: Dicing, Laser Annealing, Test | Dymek\nPower Semiconductors\n# SiC and GaN Power Device Manufacturing: Key Process, Test and Inspection Challenges\nWide-bandgap power devices are at the heart of EV inverters and fast chargers. Their materials make every process step more demanding. This article outlines where the principal challenges lie.\nSilicon carbide (SiC) and gallium nitride (GaN) can handle higher voltages, temperatures and switching speeds than silicon. This makes them central to EV traction inverters, on-board chargers, fast-charging stations and efficient power supplies. Manufacturing them, however, is more demanding at almost every step.\n## Why wide-bandgap materials are more demanding\n- SiC is extremely hard and brittle. Its hardness approaches that of diamond, which makes grinding, polishing and dicing slow and wear-intensive.\n- Crystal defects are more significant. Defects in SiC substrates and epitaxy can cause device failure or gradual degradation over time.\n- GaN is often grown on other substrates , such as silicon, which introduces stress, wafer bow and defect-related challenges.\n- Devices operate at high temperatures and voltages , so packages and interconnects are subject to severe thermal and electrical stress.\n## Key process challenges\nWafer thinning and dicing. Thinner wafers reduce on-resistance, but thin SiC is fragile. Blade dicing of SiC is slow and may cause chipping. Laser-based separation methods can improve edge quality, processing speed and mechanical strength.\nBackside contacts. After thinning, SiC devices require a low-resistance ohmic contact on the backside. Selective laser annealing can form these contacts whilst limiting the heat reaching the finished front-side structures.\nThermal and plasma processing. High-temperature steps, together with controlled plasma etching and deposition, form the device structures and passivation.\nThin-film metrology. Film thickness and film properties must be carefully controlled across the wafer.\n## Test and inspection\nWafer inspection. Automated optical inspection identifies surface defects and process abnormalities at an early stage, before further value is added.\nHigh-voltage, high-current wafer test. Wafer-level probing across a range of controlled temperatures characterises device performance and reliability prior to packaging.\nPackage inspection and failure analysis. Power modules employ sintered die attach, thick wire bonds and large solder areas. X-ray, CT and scanning acoustic microscopy are used to detect voids and delamination. High-resolution analysis then investigates metallisation and interconnect damage following stress testing.\n## The wider picture\nAs the EV and energy markets grow, additional wide-bandgap capacity is being built, including across Asia. Each new line requires process equipment, metrology and test capability proven on SiC and GaN, together with local engineers to support the ramp-up.\nDymek supports SiC and GaN development and production with solutions for wafer processing, laser annealing, dicing, inspection, probing and failure analysis. Explore our automotive and e-mobility solutions or speak with our team .\n## More insights\nAdvanced Packaging\n### What Is Hybrid Bonding? Wafer-to-Wafer vs Die-to-Wafer Explained\nHybrid bonding joins two chips through copper pads and dielectric at the same time, with no solder bumps. Here is how it works, where it is used and what it takes to get yield.\nPhotonics\n### Co-Packaged Optics (CPO) Explained: Why AI Data Centers Are Moving Optics onto the Package\nNvidia and Broadcom are now shipping co-packaged optics switches. Here is what CPO is, why it saves power, and what makes it hard to manufacture.\nFailure Analysis\n### How Laser Sample Preparation Speeds Up Semiconductor Failure Analysis\nFailure analysis often waits on sample preparation. Laser-based preparation removes bulk material fast, so FIB time goes to the final, precise step.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: southeast-asia-semiconductor-equipment.html | Southeast Asia Semiconductor Industry Growth: Malaysia, Thailand, Vietnam, Singapore | Dymek\nIndustry Insight\n# Southeast Asia's Semiconductor Growth: What It Means for Equipment Buyers\nSoutheast Asia is progressing from assembly towards advanced packaging, design and specialty fabs. This article considers what that shift means when selecting equipment and support.\nSoutheast Asia has formed part of the semiconductor supply chain for more than 50 years. Today, the region is moving up the value chain, from basic assembly towards advanced packaging, test, design and specialty manufacturing.\n## Malaysia: from assembly hub to advanced packaging\nIntel opened its first overseas assembly plant in Penang in 1972. Since then, Malaysia has become a global centre for back-end manufacturing. According to a 2024 article published by the Malaysian Investment Development Authority (MIDA):\n- Malaysia holds approximately 13% of global semiconductor testing and packaging .\n- The industry accounts for approximately 25% of Malaysia's GDP .\n- The National Semiconductor Strategy begins with RM25 billion in public funding. It aims to attract RM500 billion of investment, train 60,000 chip engineers and develop at least 10 Malaysian companies in design and advanced packaging.\n## A wider regional shift\nAcross the region, manufacturers are diversifying their supply chains and investing in new capacity. Singapore has well-established strengths in wafer fabrication and equipment. Thailand, Vietnam and the Philippines are expanding their electronics, assembly and test activities. Together, they form a connected manufacturing network.\n## What this means for equipment buyers\nMoving up the value chain changes what manufacturers require from their equipment and suppliers.\n1. More advanced processes. Advanced packaging, hybrid bonding, fine-pitch assembly and photonics require tighter process control and more capable metrology.\n2. Faster ramps. New lines must achieve yield quickly. Feasibility trials and application support before purchase help to reduce risk.\n3. Local service is important. When a tool goes down, waiting for an engineer to fly in results in lost output. Local field service, spare parts and training help to shorten downtime.\n4. Multi-site support. Many companies operate plants in several countries. A supplier with offices across the region is able to support all of them consistently.\n5. Data and yield. As products become more complex, linking inspection, metrology and test data becomes central to yield learning.\n## How Dymek fits in\nSince 1989, Dymek has connected manufacturers across Asia with advanced equipment from Europe, the USA, Japan and South Korea. We maintain offices in Hong Kong, mainland China, Taiwan, Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand, Vietnam and the Philippines. In 2023, EV Group and Dymek formed a joint venture in Penang to strengthen local installation, service, spare parts and process support.\nExplore solutions for your industry or contact your nearest office .\n## Sources\n- MIDA: Time for Malaysia to keep its edge in global chips (July 2024)\n- EV Group: EVG and Dymek form joint venture in Malaysia\n## More insights\nAdvanced Packaging\n### What Is Hybrid Bonding? Wafer-to-Wafer vs Die-to-Wafer Explained\nHybrid bonding joins two chips through copper pads and dielectric at the same time, with no solder bumps. Here is how it works, where it is used and what it takes to get yield.\nPhotonics\n### Co-Packaged Optics (CPO) Explained: Why AI Data Centers Are Moving Optics onto the Package\nNvidia and Broadcom are now shipping co-packaged optics switches. Here is what CPO is, why it saves power, and what makes it hard to manufacture.\nFailure Analysis\n### How Laser Sample Preparation Speeds Up Semiconductor Failure Analysis\nFailure analysis often waits on sample preparation. Laser-based preparation removes bulk material fast, so FIB time goes to the final, precise step.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: thin-film-thickness-measurement.html | Thin Film Thickness Measurement Methods Compared: Reflectometry, Ellipsometry, Interferometry | Dymek\nMetrology\n# Thin Film Thickness Measurement: Reflectometry vs Ellipsometry vs Interferometry\nThree optical methods are predominantly used for thin-film measurement. This article explains what each one measures, where it performs best and how to select the most suitable approach.\nFilm thickness has a direct bearing on the performance of semiconductors, optical coatings, displays, solar cells and medical devices. Most production teams measure it optically, as optical methods are both fast and non-contact. The three principal options are spectral reflectometry, spectroscopic ellipsometry and optical interferometry.\n## Spectral reflectometry\nHow it works. Light is directed onto the film at or near normal incidence. Reflections from the upper and lower surfaces of the film interfere, producing a pattern of peaks and troughs across the wavelength range. Software then fits this spectrum to a model to determine thickness and, frequently, the refractive index (n) and extinction coefficient (k).\nStrengths\n- Fast, straightforward and cost-effective\n- Measures from a few nanometres up to very thick films, depending on the system\n- Readily automated for mapping and production checks\n- Small-spot versions are able to measure patterned regions\nLimitations\n- Very thin films and complex stacks are more difficult to separate\n- Requires a reasonable knowledge of the materials\nTypical applications. Resists, oxides, nitrides, hard coats, AR coatings, polymer films and production monitoring.\n## Spectroscopic ellipsometry\nHow it works. Light strikes the sample at an oblique angle. The instrument measures the change in polarisation on reflection, expressed as two values, Psi and Delta, across the wavelength range. A model is then used to extract thickness and optical constants.\nStrengths\n- Highly sensitive to ultra-thin films, down to sub-nanometre changes\n- Measures n and k directly, including those of unknown materials\n- Well suited to complex multilayer stacks\nLimitations\n- Instruments and models are more complex\n- Slower and more costly than reflectometry\n- Larger measurement spot at oblique angles\nTypical applications. Gate dielectrics, ultra-thin layers, materials research and optical constant libraries.\n## Optical interferometry\nHow it works. Interferometers compare light reflected from the sample with that from a reference. They measure surface height, flatness and form. White-light and coherence-scanning methods are also able to resolve the thickness of transparent films and buried interfaces.\nStrengths\n- Full-field 3D topography, rather than a single point\n- Measures step heights, warpage, roughness and surface form\n- Dynamic interferometry is able to operate in vibration-prone environments, close to the process\nLimitations\n- Thickness measurement is dependent on film transparency and contrast\n- Different instruments are suited to different scales\nTypical applications. Optical surfaces, wafer flatness, MEMS, microlens arrays and package warpage.\n## Comparison at a glance\nThickness, n, k\n3D surface, form, step height\nGood\nExcellent\nLimited\nVery fast\nModerate\nFast, full-field\nStraightforward\nRequires expertise\nModerate\nProduction checks\nResearch and thin stacks\nSurfaces and topography\n## Selecting a method\nBegin by considering three questions:\n- What information is required? Thickness alone, optical constants, or surface shape?\n- How thin, and how complex, is the film stack?\n- Where will measurement take place? In a laboratory, beside the line, or inline?\nMany fabs employ more than one method: ellipsometry to build accurate material models, reflectometry for fast production mapping, and interferometry for surface and warpage measurement.\nDymek supplies thin-film metrology, interferometry and 3D surface measurement across the semiconductor, optics, display and solar sectors. View our metrology solutions or request an application review .\n## Sources\n- 4D Technology: dynamic interferometry\n## More insights\nAdvanced Packaging\n### What Is Hybrid Bonding? Wafer-to-Wafer vs Die-to-Wafer Explained\nHybrid bonding joins two chips through copper pads and dielectric at the same time, with no solder bumps. Here is how it works, where it is used and what it takes to get yield.\nPhotonics\n### Co-Packaged Optics (CPO) Explained: Why AI Data Centers Are Moving Optics onto the Package\nNvidia and Broadcom are now shipping co-packaged optics switches. Here is what CPO is, why it saves power, and what makes it hard to manufacture.\nFailure Analysis\n### How Laser Sample Preparation Speeds Up Semiconductor Failure Analysis\nFailure analysis often waits on sample preparation. Laser-based preparation removes bulk material fast, so FIB time goes to the final, precise step.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.\n\n=== PAGE: wafer-processing-device-fabrication.html | Wafer Processing & Device Fabrication Equipment: Lithography, Bonding, Etch | Dymek\n# Wafer Processing & Device Fabrication Equipment\nDevice fabrication depends on tight control of patterning, deposition, etching, thermal treatment, bonding and cleaning. Dymek provides process equipment and engineering support from wafer-level R&D through pilot and volume manufacturing.\nWe work across silicon, MEMS, sensors, compound semiconductors, SiC/GaN power devices and optoelectronics.\n- 01 Lithography & Resist Processing We provide lithography and resist-processing solutions for pattern definition at wafer level. Depending on feature size, substrate and production needs, this can include: Resist coating and developing Mask alignment and maskless exposure Nanoimprint lithography These technologies support conventional semiconductor patterning. They also suit MEMS, advanced packaging, photonics and other applications that need flexible pattern transfer or non-standard substrates.\n- Resist coating and developing\n- Mask alignment and maskless exposure\n- Nanoimprint lithography\n- 02 Wafer Bonding, Layer Transfer & Substrate Engineering Wafer bonding makes it possible to manufacture MEMS, engineered substrates, image sensors, power devices and compound semiconductor structures. These devices rely on permanent or temporary joining of wafers and layers. Bonding capabilities include fusion/direct and plasma-activated bonding, temporary bonding and debonding, thin-wafer handling, layer transfer and aligned bonding. Low-temperature bonding supports heterogeneous integration of dissimilar semiconductor materials and engineered substrates. For MEMS and sensors, aligned bonding can also create hermetic or high-vacuum cavity encapsulation. This is used for inertial and environmental sensors, microbolometers, microphones, ultrasonic devices and related structures.\n- 03 Plasma Etch, Deposition & Surface Processing Plasma processing forms device structures, opens features, deposits functional films and modifies surfaces. We provide equipment for dry etching, plasma-enhanced deposition and surface treatment on silicon, dielectric, compound semiconductor and other materials. Applications include MEMS structures, power devices, sensors, photonic devices, microLEDs and other devices that need controlled anisotropic etching or deposited films. Vacuum deposition goes beyond conventional metallization. It also covers piezoelectric films for MEMS and BAW/FBAR devices, sensor oxides, contact and barrier layers for GaN and SiC, PIC films and specialized materials for emerging quantum devices.\n- 04 Thermal Processing & Furnaces Thermal process equipment for oxidation, diffusion, annealing, LPCVD and other high-temperature wafer processes. Configurations suit university and R&D laboratories as well as production environments. Thermal processing is used in silicon device fabrication, MEMS, power semiconductors, photovoltaic devices and specialized material systems. Refurbishment and upgrade support may also be available for selected legacy furnaces.\n- 05 Wafer Separation, Dicing & Laser Processing We support wafer separation and dicing for silicon, SiC and other substrates. The process is chosen by material, device structure, edge quality, particle control, mechanical strength and throughput. Precision laser processing adds local annealing, trimming, scribing and structuring. For power semiconductors, selective laser annealing can form ohmic contacts on SiC while limiting heat impact on structures already built. Similar approaches serve magnetic sensors and other specialty devices.\n- 06 CMP Process Development & Consumables Chemical mechanical polishing (CMP) needs coordinated control of slurry chemistry, pad condition, downforce, speed and conditioning. We support CMP process development and consumables evaluation for semiconductor, MEMS, compound semiconductor, optical and advanced-material substrates. Laboratory-scale systems let you: Compare slurries, pads and conditioners Optimize removal rate and uniformity Correlate friction or process conditions with changes in pad and wafer surfaces before recipes move to production\n- Compare slurries, pads and conditioners\n- Optimize removal rate and uniformity\n- Correlate friction or process conditions with changes in pad and wafer surfaces before recipes move to production\n- 07 Process Control & Thin-Film Metrology Process development and production need reliable measurement of deposited and patterned layers. Optical metrology measures film thickness, refractive index, extinction coefficient, reflectance, transmittance and multilayer structures for dielectrics, resists, semiconductor layers and coatings. Non-contact 3D and thickness metrology adds process control for patterned photoresist, transparent functional layers and microfabricated features. This includes post-development and pre-etch checks on silicon, GaAs, MEMS, LED and VCSEL structures. Wafer-level optical metrology also measures flatness, thickness variation and surface finish on polished or patterned substrates. Use it for incoming qualification, polishing control and process monitoring. Non-standard devices can be evaluated through feasibility trials and custom process integration.\n### Not sure which technology fits?\nSend us your material, feature size and target spec. We’ll suggest a method and, where possible, a feasibility test.\n## Explore connected areas\nBack-end\n### Advanced Packaging & Assembly\nHybrid bonding, wafer-to-wafer and die-to-wafer bonding, temporary bonding/debonding, die placement, thermocompression and more.\nYield & quality\n### Metrology, Inspection & Failure Analysis\nThin-film metrology, wafer AOI, X-ray/CT, scanning acoustic microscopy, probing, SEM/TEM and more.\nSolar\n### Photovoltaics & Solar Manufacturing\nDiffusion furnaces, PVD and plasma deposition, laser contact opening, thermal laser separation for half-cells and more.\n## Tell us about your application\nShare your part, process or specification. Our engineers will recommend a practical next step, from a feasibility test to a full proposal.";
